        "h": "What this chapter was owed, what it compressed, and what is not here",
        "body": "### ⚠️ The split, recorded\n- **Printed at 17 pp:** the chapter was written whole against **both decks**, spliced and printed — **four pages past the ≤13 hard shape**.\n- **The seam:** the **DECK BOUNDARY**, contiguous and clean. **`L5) Muscle Disease` here**; **`L6) Peripheral neuropathy` in `nr-nerve`**.\n- **Why it is allowed:** **two topics**, not one topic taught over two lectures, so **CLAUDE.md §4's merge rule does not bind**.\n- **⚠️ It reverses a recorded merge:** the original **`nr-lmn1` + `nr-lmn2`** join at the head of `app\\data\\modules.js`. **SCHEDULE is untouched**, per §4 and the `nr-hemi`/`nr-para` precedent.\n- **The single question stays here**, in `lm-3`.\n\n### ⚠️ Register (§14.5) — four rows RECEIVED from three donors, all delivered\n\n|**Owed by**|**Fact**|**Delivered**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**`nr-stroke`** *(str-12)*|the **UMN/LMN framework**, and **LMN disease as a subject**|✅ **`lm-1`**, and the two chapters entire|\n|**`nr-hemi`** *(hem-16)*|the **UMN/LMN discrimination as a framework**|✅ **`lm-1`** — the bedside table|\n|**`nr-hemi`** *(hem-16)*|**PERIPHERAL NEUROPATHY, MYASTHENIA GRAVIS, MUSCULAR DYSTROPHY, MOTOR NEURON DISEASE and GUILLAIN-BARRÉ as diseases**, plus **nerve conduction studies and EMG** as techniques|✅ `lm-7`-`lm-9` · `lm-2`-`lm-6` · **`lm-6`, supplied and tagged** · **`lm-1`**. ⚠️ **Peripheral neuropathy and GBS are delivered in `nr-nerve`**|\n|**`nr-cranial`** *(cn-12)*|the **UMN/LMN framework in general** — limbs, tone, reflexes, plantars, wasting|✅ **`lm-1`** — all four rows|\n\n- **⚠️ The framework:** it is **not in this chapter's own deck**. `UMN` returns in **L1, L2, L8, L10, L12 and L17** and **not in L5 or L6**, verified by grep across all 25 caches. It is written from **L1**, which is **`nr-intro`'s** deck — so **those two slides are counted here and `nr-intro` must not count them again**.\n- **Not repeated here:** the **UMN and LMN SITE LISTS** are in **`hem-7`**, and the **facial application** in **`str-4`** and **`cn-9`**. **POLIOMYELITIS as a disease** is already filed by `nr-hemi` to **`nr-cns`**.\n\n### ⚠️ One compression, recorded\n- **The MGFA classification:** a **~250-word verbatim slide**, carried in `lm-7` as **a table on its two axes**. **Every class I-V and both sub-classes a/b survive**, as does the feeding-tube rule; the **repeated boilerplate** — the identical *may also have ocular muscle weakness of any severity* clause on four classes — does not. **No fact is dropped.**\n\n### Deck quality — measured, not assumed\n- **The page audit:** **12 of L5's 55 pages carry fewer than 12 extractable words**.\n- **⚠️ The five zero-text pages carry ZERO FONTS**, checked with `pdffonts` page by page, **so no text is hidden as vector outlines**. Four are **genuine picture slides**; **p42 has no fonts AND no images — it is blank**.\n- **Nothing was rendered.** The picture pages are the **Gowers' sign**, **calf pseudohypertrophy with tendo-Achilles contracture** and **exaggerated lordosis** figures — and **every one is captioned in the extracted text**, which is where `lm-3` took them from.\n\n### What this deck does not contain\n- **Named and not described:** **dermatomyositis**, **polymyositis**, **inclusion body myopathy**, the **congenital myopathies**, **glycogen and lipid storage disease**, **mitochondrial myopathy** and the **periodic paralyses**. They exist in `lm-2`'s classification and nowhere else in the module.\n- **No thymectomy indications and no timing** — the operation is listed, unqualified, in `lm-9`.\n- **⚠️ No ranking anywhere.** Nothing is called commonest, and the dystrophies are never ordered by frequency.\n- **Incidence appears twice only:** **Duchenne, 20-30 per 100,000 live-born males**, and **myasthenia, about 1 in 100,000**.\n\nSrc: L5 read end to end; the page audit is a `pdftotext` / `pdffonts` / `pdfimages` sweep of all 55 pages, 2026-08-22. The register rows are quoted from `str-12`, `hem-16` and `cn-12` in app\\data\\theory.neuro.js on disk",
