        "h": "Status epilepticus — the emergency protocol by the clock",
        "body": "- **⚠️ THE DEFINITION:** a **SEIZURE LASTING 5 MINUTES OR MORE**, **OR TWO OR MORE SEIZURES WITHOUT RECOVERY** between them. **⚠️ BEYOND 30 MINUTES there is a RISK OF PERMANENT NEURONAL INJURY.**\n\n|**Time**|**Step**|**Detail**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**0-5 min**|**ABC**|**AIRWAY · BREATHING · CIRCULATION**. **O₂**, **IV ACCESS**, **GLUCOSE CHECK**, **ECG**|\n|**5-10 min**|**DIAZEPAM**|**IV 10 mg in adults** / **0.1-0.3 mg/kg in children**, **OVER 15 SECONDS**|\n|**10-30 min**|**PHENYTOIN**|**IV 1000 mg adults** / **18-20 mg/kg children**, **SLOWLY OVER 20 MINUTES**. ⚠️ **OR LEVETIRACETAM 3000 mg IV if phenytoin is unavailable**|\n|**over 30 min**|**REFRACTORY**|**IV THIOPENTAL / PROPOFOL / MIDAZOLAM INFUSION**. **ICU ADMISSION** with **EEG MONITORING**. **AIM FOR BURST SUPPRESSION**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ THE ROUTE RULE:** in the deck's own words — **ONLY THE IV ROUTE in convulsive status. NEVER oral or IM during active convulsions** — **unless there is no IV access, in which case RECTAL DIAZEPAM or IM MIDAZOLAM.**\n- **⚠️ The deck's summary of the whole protocol:** ***A-B-C → Diazepam IV → Phenytoin IV → ICU. Time is neurons.***\n- **Read the clock as the point.** Each box is **a time window, not a preference**: the benzodiazepine belongs in the first ten minutes and the loading agent in the first thirty, because **the injury threshold is 30 minutes**.\n\nSrc: L4 sl. STATUS EPILEPTICUS — EMERGENCY PROTOCOL · KEY CLINICAL TAKEAWAYS (item 04)",
