        "id": "ax-5",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Panic disorder",
        "body": "- **The disorder is RECURRENT EPISODES** of panic attacks.\n- **A panic attack is:** **SUDDEN ONSET** of **intense fear or discomfort**, usually **reaching a PEAK WITHIN 10 MINUTES**.\n- **With at least FOUR of thirteen:** palpitations, pounding heart or accelerated rate · sweating · trembling or shaking · shortness of breath or smothering · **choking sensation** · chest pain or discomfort · nausea or abdominal distress · dizzy, unsteady, lightheaded or faint · **derealization** (feeling of unreality) **or depersonalization** (detachment from oneself) · **fear of losing control or going crazy** · **fear of dying** · paraesthesia · chills or hot flushes.\n- **⚠️ What makes it a DISORDER:** the patient **fears recurrent attacks** and **worries about the implications** — losing control, going crazy, dying — producing **significant behavioural change**, typically **avoiding situations or locations**. **Attacks alone are not panic disorder.**\n\n### The cycle the book draws — and it explains the treatment\n- **Four symptoms at the centre:** **palpitations · sweating · shortness of breath · dizziness**.\n- **Two arcs enclose them:** **ANTICIPATORY ANXIETY** above — *fear of future attacks* — and **BEHAVIOURAL AVOIDANCE** below — *avoiding triggers and situations*.\n- **⚠️ It is a loop:** attack → anticipation → avoidance, and **the avoidance prevents the disconfirmation that would end it**. That is what the exposure methods in `ax-8` are aimed at.\n\n### STUDENTS FEAR the 3Cs — the book's second mnemonic\n- **S**weating · **T**rembling · **U**nsteadiness · **D**epersonalization · **E**levated rate · **N**ausea · **T**ingling · **S**hortness of breath · **FEAR** of dying or losing control · **the 3 Cs — Chest pain · Choking · Chills/hot flushes**.\n\n### ⚠️ What the patient has to be told — and it is written elsewhere\n- **`ps-psychoed`'s `pe-2`** carries the psychoeducation in full, as **Myth #2**. Two lines of it are clinical rather than educational and belong here: attacks **resolve, typically in 10-20 minutes**, and **the differential at the door is a cardiac event** — panic **peaks quickly and resolves fully**. **Catastrophic misinterpretation perpetuates the cycle**, which is the same loop drawn above.\n- **⚠️ Two 10-minute figures, not one:** the book's is time to **PEAK**; `L6`'s 10-20 minutes is time to **RESOLVE**. They are complementary, not contradictory.\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.3 printed p.196 — criteria, the unnumbered panic-cycle figure and the STUDENTS FEAR the 3Cs poster; L6 sl. MYTH #2 for the resolution time and the cardiac differential, written in full in `ps-psychoed`",
