        "h": "The four phases",
        "body": "|**Phase**|**What it is**|\n|---|---|\n|**1. PREMORBID** *(at-risk state)*|**functionality PRIOR to onset.** Includes **at-risk mental state (ARMS)** subjects, who act **odd or eccentric** and **avoid relationships** — e.g. **paranoid and schizotypal** personalities. **⚠️ Only 30 % go on to be schizophrenic**|\n|**2. PRODROMAL** *(early signs)*|**transient or self-limiting PSYCHOTIC symptoms**, plus non-specific ones: **social withdrawal, irritability, dysphoria, anxiety, hypochondriasis, clumsiness**|\n|**3. ACTIVE** *(diagnosis)*|the period where **the diagnosis is made**, on **DSM-5 or ICD-10** criteria|\n|**4. RESIDUAL** *(negative symptoms)*|**only negative symptoms remain** — e.g. flat affect, lack of motivation|\n\n- **⚠️ The 30 % appears TWICE:** and means the same thing both times — here for the premorbid phase, and in `sz-3` for the hyperdopaminergic *transition group*. **They are the same population**, described once clinically and once biochemically.\n- **Which criteria are used where:** **ICD-10** (WHO) is typical in **European** countries; **DSM-5** (APA) in the **United States**, to varying degrees around the world, and it is **prevailing in research studies**.\n- **⚠️ ICD-10 — named twice, never printed:** its criteria appear nowhere in the chapter. Only DSM-5's are given, in `sz-6`. **A list is not coverage.**\n- **⚠️ The residual phase gets a five-word body** — the thinnest of the four. **No duration, no proportion and no management is printed for it**, which matters because it is the phase most patients spend most of their lives in. *(That last clause is mine, not the book's.)*\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7 printed p.231 — the unnumbered `PHASES OF SCHIZOPHRENIA` poster and the `Phases` prose beneath it, which agree throughout",
