        "h": "Course — and two sources that count it differently",
        "body": "- **⚠️ Rank:** schizophrenia is a major cause of **disability**, with **ACTIVE PSYCHOSIS the THIRD-MOST-DISABLING condition** — **after quadriplegia and dementia**.\n- **Life expectancy** is lower than the general population's, from **poor general health** and **high comorbidities** — the 10-to-25-year figure is in `ps-psychosis`'s `sz-1`.\n\n### The book's course grading — first-admission patients, over 5 years\n|**%**|**Course pattern, as printed**|\n|---|---|\n|**22 %**|**one episode only — no impairment**|\n|**35 %**|**several episodes with no or minimal impairment**|\n|**8 %**|**impairment after the first episode**, with subsequent exacerbation and **no return to normality**|\n|**38 %**|**impairment increasing with each of several episodes**, and no return to normality|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ THOSE FOUR PERCENTAGES SUM TO 103 %.** 22 + 35 + 8 + 38. **Recorded as printed, not reconciled and not adjusted** — the error is in the source, and correcting it silently would hide it.\n- **⚠️ Read the table by baseline — not by episode count.** Rows 2 and 4 both have several episodes; what separates them is whether the patient **returns to normality between them**. **57 % end with no lasting impairment, 46 % with impairment** — and yes, those also sum past 100.\n\n### ⚠️ The deck counts in thirds instead\n|**Deck**|**Outcome**|\n|---|---|\n|**1/3**|**significant recovery**|\n|**1/3**|**partial recovery**|\n|**1/3**|**chronic illness with significant disability**|\n\n- **⚠️ The two schemes are not reconcilable — and neither is corrected.** The deck's thirds are the classical teaching figure; the book's four rows are a graded first-admission study. **They agree only in direction** — roughly half do well, roughly half do not. **Quote whichever source a question is written from.**\n- **⚠️ The deck's `Prognostic predictors:` slide is a HEADING WITH NO BODY** — a title and a YouTube link, no text. **`sm-2` fills it from the book.**\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7 printed p.233 — Course & Prognosis, and the unnumbered four-row course panel; L11,12 sl. Long-Term Outcomes · Prognostic predictors",
