        "body": "|**✓ GOOD prognosis**|**✗ POOR prognosis**|\n|---|---|\n|**old age of onset**|**young age of onset**|\n|**female**|**male**|\n|**married**|**unmarried**|\n|**no family history**|**family history**|\n|**good premorbid personality**|**personality problems**|\n|**high IQ**|**low IQ**|\n|**precipitation** *(i.e. a precipitating factor present)*|**no obvious precipitants**|\n|**POSITIVE symptoms**|**NEGATIVE symptoms**|\n|**treatment compliance**|**poor treatment compliance**|\n|**good support**|**low support**|\n|**acute onset**|**insidious onset**|\n|**presence of a mood component**|**no mood component**|\n\n- **⚠️ `Precipitation`:** the good column prints that single word where the poor column prints *no obvious precipitants*. It means **a precipitating factor was present**. **Recorded as printed.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ All of it is inside this picture:** the **entire prognostic content of the chapter**. There is **no prose list of prognostic factors anywhere** in the eleven pages. **A reader who skips posters gets nothing on prognosis** — and this is the second time in the chapter that a poster carries content the prose never states, after the structural pathology in `sz-2`.\n- **⚠️ Two rows restate earlier work:** **positive against negative symptoms** is `sz-7`'s response-to-medication asymmetry read as an outcome; **presence of a mood component** is the criterion C boundary in `sz-6` seen from the other side — the closer a case sits to schizoaffective disorder, the better it does.\n- **⚠️ Twelve rows, thirteen paired items** — the poster is printed as thirteen rows in the source and one pair is a heading. **Count the pairs, not the rules.**\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7 printed p.234 — the unnumbered full-width GOOD/POOR PROGNOSIS comparison poster occupying the top three-quarters of the page",
