        "h": "Antipsychotics — three classes and one receptor number",
        "body": "- **First-line treatment**, and it **reduces POSITIVE symptoms in 7 TO 14 DAYS**.\n- **Continued use decreases the risk of RELAPSE.**\n- **⚠️ Efficacy has a number:** it requires **D2 RECEPTOR OCCUPANCY of optimally 65 %** in the **ASSOCIATIVE STRIATUM**, for an adequate portion of time. **That is the same pathway `sz-3` blames for positive symptoms** — the drug is aimed exactly where the excess is.\n- **Choice of agent is by BENEFITS — RISKS and COSTS.** The book gives no algorithm beyond that.\n\n|**Class**|**Agents named**|**Mechanism**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Typical**|**haloperidol** · **chlorpromazine**|**dopamine antagonist** — **not particularly selective**; blocking **D2** is thought to produce some of the unwanted effects|\n|**Atypical**|**risperidone** · **olanzapine** · **clozapine**|**dopamine AND serotonin antagonist** — a similar D2 block, but most **also act on 5-HT2A and 5-HT2C**|\n|**3rd generation**|**aripiprazole**|**PARTIAL dopamine agonist-antagonist**, or *dopamine stabilizer*|\n\n- **⚠️ The prose gives no mechanism — for the 3rd generation.** Its bullet list has **two entries for three classes**; the partial-agonist mechanism exists **only in the marginal picture and in the classification line**. **Read the figures.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ QUETIAPINE:** named, and **never classified**. It appears in this chapter **only inside the hyperprolactinaemia and weight-gain clauses** of `sm-7`, without ever being assigned to a class. **Seven agents are named in the chapter; six are classified.** In book ch.11 it appears as a **mood stabiliser**, which is a different chapter's use, not a classification here.\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ NO DOSE IS PRINTED FOR ANY OF THE SEVEN\n- **No dose in any form: no mg, no mg/kg, no frequency and no serum level appears anywhere in the eleven pages.** **ROUTE is the one prescribing dimension printed**, and only as a formulation contrast — **long-acting depot against oral** — with **no route attached to any individual agent**.\n\n|**Supplied starting/maintenance ranges**|**Typical adult oral dose** *(not taken from the course material)*|\n|---|---|\n|**Haloperidol**|**2-10 mg/day**|\n|**Chlorpromazine**|**100-600 mg/day**|\n|**Risperidone**|**2-6 mg/day**|\n|**Olanzapine**|**5-20 mg/day**|\n|**Quetiapine**|**300-750 mg/day**|\n|**Aripiprazole**|**10-30 mg/day**|\n|**Clozapine**|**titrated slowly to 300-450 mg/day** — **reserved for resistance**, see `sm-8`|\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7 printed p.236 — Medication (Antipsychotics), Classification, Mechanism of action, and the three marginal figure panels read at 300 dpi. The dose table is supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4; the zero-dose finding is the cache's own exhaustive sweep of the chapter",
