        "h": "Adverse effects — common, and the one that is rare",
        "body": "### 1. Common — **≥ 1 %, and up to 50 % incidence for most antipsychotics**\n|**Effect**|**Mechanism and detail**|\n|---|---|\n|**EXTRAPYRAMIDAL (EPS)**|blocking **D2 at the NIGROSTRIATAL pathway**, **particularly common on FIRST-GENERATION** drugs. Four named: **AKATHISIA** — *an often-distressing sense of inner restlessness* — **dystonia**, **parkinsonism**, **tremor**|\n|**TARDIVE DYSKINESIA**|**slow, repetitive, involuntary and purposeless movements** of **face and lips**; appears after **CHRONIC use**, **frequently IRREVERSIBLE**, **tends to RESIST treatment**. More frequent with **typical** (haloperidol), but it **can occur with the others**|\n|**HYPERPROLACTINAEMIA**|blocking **D2 at the TUBEROINFUNDIBULAR pathway** — **rare with clozapine, quetiapine and aripiprazole**. Causes **galactorrhoea**, **gynaecomastia**, **sexual dysfunction in both sexes**, **osteoporosis**|\n|**ANTICHOLINERGIC**|**common with olanzapine and clozapine, less likely on risperidone**: **blurred vision**, **constipation**, **dry mouth** — **⚠️ although HYPERSALIVATION may also occur with clozapine** — and **reduced perspiration**|\n\n- **⚠️ Two pathways — two adverse effects, and both are in `sz-3`.** **EPS is the nigrostriatal block**; **hyperprolactinaemia is the tuberoinfundibular block**, because dopamine's job there is to **inhibit** prolactin. **The mechanism table predicts the side-effect table.**\n- **⚠️ EPS treatment: one heading, two drug CLASSES:** **anticholinergic medications and propranolol**. **No agent, no dose, no route, no frequency** — and it is the chapter’s only statement of EPS treatment. *(Propranolol is the one that treats akathisia specifically — supplied.)*\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ A structural defect on printed 237 — recorded, NOT reconciled\n- **Two extra bullets:** they sit **inside the hyperprolactinaemia list** — **HYPOTENSION** and **WEIGHT GAIN** *(prominent in clozapine, olanzapine, quetiapine)*. The block then closes: *the proposed mechanism is antagonizing the **Histamine H1 and Serotonin 5HT2C** receptors.*\n- **⚠️ That is not a prolactin mechanism.** Either the two bullets and the closing sentence are a **separate adverse-effect block that lost its heading**, or the list is **mis-scoped**. **Transcribed exactly as printed.**\n- **⚠️ And the error — if it is one, is CONSISTENT** — the printed-237 poster also files **WEIGHT GAIN** under hyperprolactinaemia. **It is not a typographical slip in the prose.** *(H1 and 5HT2C antagonism is the standard explanation for antipsychotic weight gain and sedation — supplied, and it is why the sentence looks displaced rather than wrong.)*\n\n### 2. Rare or uncommon but severe — **< 1 %**\n- **NEUROLEPTIC MALIGNANT SYNDROME**, and the chapter's entire treatment of it is the parenthesis ***(refer to psychiatric emergency)***. **See `sm-5` — it is taught in `L15` and `L16`, and belongs to `ps-emerg`.**\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7 printed p.237 — Adverse effects in full, and the ADVERSE EFFECTS poster read at 300 dpi. Three parenthesised readings are supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4 and are marked where they occur",
