        "h": "How each antidepressant class works",
        "body": "|**Class**|**Mechanism, as the sources give it**|\n|---|---|\n|**SSRIs**|**block SERT** at the **somatodendritic** end. ⚠️ **The deck's caption is the mechanism worth quoting:** *the **downregulation of the autoreceptors** causes the neuron to release **more 5HT at the axon***. **The 5HT1A autoreceptor is the delay** — which is why `ps-anxiety`'s onset is **2-8 weeks** and not two days *(that link is mine)*|\n|**TCAs**|**inhibit reuptake of serotonin AND noradrenaline** by binding **SERT and NAT**, raising both in the synaptic cleft. ⚠️ **And they block three more receptors** — **muscarinic**, **H1** and **alpha** — which the deck labels straight onto **orthostatic hypotension**. **`ph-4`'s side-effect list is that receptor list read downwards**|\n|**SNRIs**|**dual reuptake inhibition**, 5-HT + NE|\n|**NDRI**|**NE and DA reuptake inhibition** — the only class that touches dopamine|\n|**NaSSAs**|a **specific antagonist**, not a reuptake inhibitor|\n|**MAOIs**|**monoamine oxidase inhibition**. ⚠️ **Neither source draws it, and neither explains the tyramine reaction** the book's own poster warns about *(the mechanism — MAO-A in the gut normally degrades dietary tyramine, so inhibition allows a hypertensive crisis — is supplied)*|\n\n- **⚠️ Selectivity is the whole story:** **TCAs hit two transporters and three receptor families; SSRIs hit one transporter.** Same efficacy target, far fewer collisions — and every TCA-specific adverse effect in `ph-4` comes from a receptor the SSRI never touches.\n\nSrc: L17 sl.7 (SSRI mechanism, with the autoreceptor caption printed inside the image) · sl.8-9 (two pasted TCA mechanism diagrams, watermarked *psych scene* and *PHARMACY FREAK*); the class mechanism glyphs are from the printed-261 poster of neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.11. The two parenthesised readings are supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
