        "id": "ph-7",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Antipsychotics — eleven agents the book never names",
        "body": "- **Also known — as NEUROLEPTICS or MAJOR TRANQUILIZERS.**\n- **Six indications — and the two sources print them identically:** **1. schizophrenia · 2. schizoaffective disorder · 3. bipolar disorder · 4. psychotic depression · 5. TREATMENT-RESISTANT major depression as an ADJUNCT to standard antidepressant therapy — *and not necessarily psychotic* · 6. TICS and CHOREA.**\n- **⚠️ Indication 5 is the one that surprises**: an antipsychotic **added to an antidepressant** in a patient with **no psychosis at all**. Indication 6 is `nr-movement`'s territory.\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ Where the classes, mechanism and adverse effects are written — NOT HERE\n- **A heading with no body:** the book's **`Antipsychotics` section**. It teaches **indications only, as a picture**, then defers everything else in one red line: ***Classes, mechanism of action & adverse effect: (See \"schizophrenia\" lecture).***\n- **That deferred content is written — in `ps-schizmgmt`'s `sm-6` and `sm-7`** — three classes, receptor mechanisms, the full adverse-effect taxonomy, EPS treatment, clozapine and agranulocytosis, depot preparations. **It is not repeated here.**\n\n### But the DECK has its own table — and it is bigger\n|**Category**|**Mechanism**|**Agents**|**Generation**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**Typical — dopamine antagonist**|blocks dopamine receptors **in ALL PARTS of the brain**|**chlorpromazine · haloperidol · pimozide · fluphenazine · trifluoperazine**|**Old**|\n|**Atypical — dopamine AND serotonin antagonist**|blocks dopamine **and serotonin** receptors|**amisulpiride** *[sic]* **· clozapine · olanzapine · sertindole · zotepine · quetiapine**|**New**|\n|**Atypical — PARTIAL dopamine antagonist**|blocks **only a portion** of dopamine receptors, and also blocks serotonin|**aripiprazole**|**New**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ Eleven of the twelve are new:** they are **named nowhere in book ch.7**, which names only seven. **This table is `ps-pharm`'s own contribution and the reason the chapter is not just a pointer.** ⚠️ **And it classifies quetiapine**, which ch.7 names and never classifies (`sm-6`).\n- **⚠️ THE TABLE IS INCOMPLETE.** A **further row exists below the aripiprazole row** and is **cut off by the crop of the pasted screenshot**. At 600 dpi only the **topmost pixel-tips** survive — enough to prove a row is there in all four columns, **not enough to read one word of it**. **NOT INFERRED.**\n- **⚠️ The atypicals' extra target is 5-HT**, which is what `sm-7` blames the weight gain on. **Same receptor, two chapters, opposite valence: the serotonin block buys the lower EPS and costs the metabolic profile.**\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ THE TWO SOURCES CONTRADICT EACH OTHER ON THE PATHWAYS\n|**Symptom**|**Deck `L17` sl.19**|**Book ch.7, in `sz-3`**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**POSITIVE**|**OVERACTIVITY of the MESOLIMBIC pathway**|**EXCESS dopamine in the ASSOCIATIVE NIGROSTRIATAL pathway**|\n|**NEGATIVE and cognitive**|**MESOCORTICAL pathway DYSFUNCTION**|**DEFICIT of dopamine in the MESOLIMBIC pathway**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ These are not reconcilable — the mesolimbic pathway carries POSITIVE symptoms in one source and NEGATIVE in the other.** **Both recorded, neither corrected**, per the standing rule. *(The deck's is the conventional scheme taught almost everywhere; the book's is its own. That is a supplied judgement, not a source statement, and it does not license editing either.)*\n- **⚠️ Slide 21 — arrows only:** it gives the consequences of **first-generation D2 blockade pathway by pathway** — **nigrostriatal ↑** (a trembling hand), **mesocortical ↑** (a withdrawn child), **mesolimbic ↓** (hallucinating eyes), **tuberoinfundibular ↑** (prolactin to the breast). **The deck writes no words for any of the four**; `sm-7` words two of them.\n\nSrc: L17 sl.17 (indications) · sl.18 (the pasted classification table and its truncated row) · sl.19 (the pathway panels, watermarked PSYCHOPHARMACOLOGY INSTITUTE) · sl.20-21; neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.11 printed p.263, whose entire antipsychotic content is the indication poster and the deferral line quoted above",
