        "id": "ph-9",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Benzodiazepines — five indications and one month",
        "body": "- **⚠️⚠️ NOT MORE THAN ONE MONTH:** the deck prints it **inside the indications heading**, bold and underlined, in red — **the only duration, dose or quantity stated anywhere in 31 slides** — and the book's poster repeats it as **`MAX 1 MONTH`**.\n- **Five indications, both sources identical:** **1. panic disorder · 2. generalized anxiety disorder · 3. insomnia · 4. seizures · 5. alcohol withdrawal.**\n- **Mechanism:** the **benzodiazepine binding site is on the GAMMA subunit of the GABA-A receptor**; BDZ binds, **then GABA binds**, and **chloride flows** — **GABA → ↓ excitability**. **The order in the deck's three-panel figure is the point: the benzodiazepine does not open the channel, it makes GABA better at opening it.** *(That last clause is a reading of the figure, not a printed caption.)*\n\n|**Generic**|**Trade name, as the deck prints it**|\n|---|---|\n|**Alprazolam**|Xanax|\n|**Clonazepam**|Klonopin|\n|**Diazepam**|Valium|\n|**Lorazepam**|Ativan|\n|**Triazolam**|Halcion|\n|**Temazepam**|Restoril|\n|**Chlordiazepoxide**|Librium|\n\n- **⚠️ The deck's own caption on that slide:** ***the most commonly prescribed benzos are the most commonly abused.*** **The book names five agents; the deck names seven with trade names.**\n\n### Adverse effects — seven, and both sources print all seven\n- **Seven, in the deck's order:** **1. amnesia · 2. RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION · 3. drowsiness · 4. hypotension · 5. DISINHIBITION, especially in the ELDERLY · 6. PARADOXICAL EFFECT — increased anxiety, aggression, impulsivity and irritability · 7. TOLERANCE and DEPENDENCE.**\n- **⚠️ Item 6 is the trap.** A benzodiazepine given for anxiety that makes the patient **more** anxious, aggressive and impulsive **has not failed — it has done the paradoxical thing**, and the answer is to stop it, not to increase it. **Read it beside item 5: both are commonest at the extremes of age.**\n- **⚠️ Item 7 is the reason — for the one-month ceiling**, and it is the exception in `ps-anxiety`'s `ax-8` — the book there writes that antidepressants **won't cause addiction *except for benzodiazepines if used for a prolonged period***. **Same rule, stated in two chapters from two directions.**\n\nSrc: L17 sl.27 (indications, with the one-month ceiling printed in the heading) · sl.28 (the three-panel GABA-A mechanism figure, which spells *benzodiazapine* [sic] twice) · sl.29 (the seven agents with trade names, watermarked Maryland) · sl.30 (the seven adverse effects); neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.11 printed p.264 — the BENZODIAZEPINES quadrant of the poster and the third column of the *Notes not included in the illustration* table",
