        "id": "bp-3",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Lumbar disc prolapse",
        "body": "- **Definition:** the soft **NUCLEUS PULPOSUS herniates through a tear in the ANNULUS FIBROSUS**.\n- **Aetiology, three:** **lumbar spine degeneration** · **multiple repetitive micro-traumata**, such as lifting heavy weights · a **single major trauma**, such as a fall or a heavy lift immediately preceding the onset.\n\n### Five clinical presentations — and they are a spectrum, not a list\n|**Presentation**|**What it means**|\n|---|---|\n|**1. BACK syndrome**|**back manifestations only**|\n|**2. LEG syndrome**|neurological and mechanical manifestations in **one or both legs** along **one or more nerve roots** — **monoradiculopathy or polyradiculopathy**|\n|**3. CLASSIC back and leg syndrome**|both together|\n|**4. NEUROGENIC CLAUDICATION**|unilateral or bilateral, mono- or polyradicular manifestations **ON MOVEMENT and RELIEVED BY REST**|\n|**5. CAUDA EQUINA syndrome**|the emergency — `bp-6`|\n\n### Symptoms\n- **LOW BACK PAIN** — **dull aching**, **aggravated by movement**, **partially relieved by rest**.\n- **SCIATICA** — leg pain **in the distribution of the affected root**, **aggravated by COUGHING and SNEEZING** and **relieved by FLEXION OF KNEE AND THIGH**.\n- **Numbness or tingling** along the affected root, i.e. **dermatomal**.\n- **Motor weakness** according to the affected root.\n- **BLADDER symptoms** — **flaccid bladder, retention with overflow incontinence** — **in cauda equina syndrome**.\n- **Sexual dysfunction**, as impotence in males.\n\n### Signs — two groups, and the second is the one that localises\n|**Group**|**Signs**|\n|---|---|\n|**BACK signs**|**loss of lumbar lordosis** · local tenderness · **limitation of spinal movement** · scoliosis · **spasm of paravertebral muscle**|\n|**LEG signs** *(signs of radiculopathy)*|**motor weakness** in the muscles supplied by the compressed root · **sensory changes** along its dermatome · **DECREASED REFLEXES** according to the root · **POSITIVE straight leg raising test (SLRT)**|\n\n- **⚠️ The discriminator: cough and sneeze aggravation.** Both raise intrathecal pressure; **mechanical back pain does not behave that way**, and neither does the hip disease in `bp-5`’s differential.\n- **⚠️ Every leg sign is a ROOT sign.** Weakness, sensory change and reflex loss are all *according to the root affected* — which is why the localisation table in `bp-7` is the section this one points at.\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed pp. 159-160 — `1. Lumbar disc prolapse`, Definition, Ɛtiology, Clinical presentations, and the Symptoms and Signs lists, which run across the 159/160 page break; L11 sl. Lumbar disc prolapse · Clinical presentations · Back signs / Leg signs, which print the same",
