        "h": "Cervical spondylosis — the most common cause",
        "body": "- **How common the problem is:** **2 OUT OF 3 PEOPLE** experience neck pain at some point in life. Neck pain and arm pain — **brachialgia** — are **very common clinical problems**.\n- **⚠️ The most common cause:** **cervical spondylosis**. Both sources say so in those words, and it is the answer to the deck's own first question (`np-8`).\n\n|**Feature**|**As printed**|\n|---|---|\n|**What it is**|a **DEGENERATIVE disorder of the cervical spine** which **can compress the CORD or the NERVE ROOTS**|\n|**The morning story**|patients often **wake with a painful STIFF NECK** and a **DIFFUSE, NON-PULSATILE HEADACHE that resolves in a few hours**|\n|**Level**|most commonly **C5/6 and C6/7**, and **focal signs are likely to be at these levels**|\n|**Hands**|**wasting and weakness of the SMALL MUSCLES OF THE HANDS**, particularly **weakness of ABDUCTION OF THE LITTLE FINGER**|\n|**⚠️ Always present**|**RESTRICTED NECK MOVEMENT**, with significant spondylosis|\n|**⚠️ The alarm**|**BLADDER DYSFUNCTION**, or **long-tract signs or symptoms**, **indicates SPINAL CORD COMPRESSION**|\n\n- **⚠️ The headache is the trap.** A **non-pulsatile morning headache that clears in a few hours** is a cervical-spine complaint, not a primary headache — **`nr-headache` has no entry for it**, and the pulsatile/non-pulsatile word is what separates them.\n- **⚠️ Weak little-finger abduction:** it is **a T1 sign** *(supplied)*, and the cervical root table in `np-3` **has no T1 column** — so the chapter names the sign and does not localise it.\n- **⚠️ Cervical spondylosis gets NO Definition — Investigation or Treatment sub-structure** — it is a **flat bullet list**, unlike every other numbered topic in the chapter. **What treats it is `np-7`'s general block.**\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed p.166 — `Neck Pain And / Or Arm Pain (Brachialgia)` and `1. Cervical spondylosis`, with the healthy/spondylotic spine figure; L11 sl. NECK PAIN AND BRACHIALGIA · Cervical spondylosis, which print the same seven points. The T1 reading is supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
