        "id": "np-2",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Cervical disc prolapse",
        "body": "- **Definition:** the soft **NUCLEUS PULPOSUS herniates through a tear in the ANNULUS FIBROSUS** — the same sentence the lumbar chapter uses in `nr-backpain`'s `bp-3`.\n- **Epidemiology:** most commonly **C5-C6 and C6-C7**; usually **middle age, 30-50 YEARS**.\n\n### ⚠️ Three outcomes, decided by WHERE the disc goes\n|**Direction of prolapse**|**What is compressed**|**Result**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**POSTEROLATERAL**|**nerve root**|**RADICULOPATHY**|\n|**CENTRAL**|**cord**|**MYELOPATHY**|\n|**both**|root **and** cord|**RADICULOMYELOPATHY**|\n\n- **⚠️ That table is the chapter's central idea.** Everything below divides into root signs and cord signs, and **central means cord** here exactly as it does for the cauda equina in `bp-6`.\n\n### Symptoms\n- **Neck pain and stiffness.**\n- **⚠️ BRACHIALGIA** — pain radiating down to the arm and hand, **exacerbated by neck motion (EXTENSION)** and **relieved by ARM ABDUCTION** — the **arm abduction test**.\n- **Paraesthesia and numbness** along the affected dermatome.\n- **Motor weakness** in **long-standing** compression.\n- **Sphincteric dysfunction** in cases of **myelopathy**.\n\n### Signs, in three groups\n|**Group**|**Signs**|\n|---|---|\n|**a. NECK signs**|**loss of cervical lordosis** · local tenderness · **limitation of spinal movement** · **spasm of paravertebral muscle**|\n|**b. RADICULOPATHY**|**motor weakness** in the muscles supplied by the compressed root · **sensory changes** along its dermatome · **DECREASED reflexes** according to the root · **the distribution of pain in the upper limb varies by root**|\n|**c. ⚠️ MYELOPATHY**|**SPASTIC QUADRIPARESIS** · **HYPERREFLEXIA BELOW the level of compression** · **clumsiness and ATAXIC GAIT disturbance** · **sphincteric disturbance**|\n\n- **⚠️ Group b is LMN and group c is UMN — and that is the whole distinction.** **Decreased** reflexes at the root against **hyperreflexia below the lesion**. **The two can coexist — that is what radiculomyelopathy means.**\n- **⚠️ The neck signs are the back signs:** one word changed from `bp-3` — loss of lordosis, tenderness, limited movement, paravertebral spasm. **Only *scoliosis* is missing from the cervical list.**\n\n### Which root, and how often\n|**Disc**|**Root**|**Frequency**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**C5-C6**|**C6**|**the most common**|\n|**C6-C7**|**C7**|next|\n|**C4-C5**|**C5**|**to a lesser extent**|\n\n- **⚠️ Cervical against lumbar:** here **the root takes the number of the LOWER vertebra** — C5-C6 hits C6. **In the lumbar spine it takes the number below it** (`bp-6`): L5-S1 hits S1. **The rule looks different because cervical roots exit above their vertebra and lumbar roots below** *(supplied — the book states the frequencies and never the reason)*.\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed pp. 166-167 — `2. Cervical disc prolapse`, Definition, Ɛpidemiology, Clinical picture running across the page break, Symptoms, Signs a-c, and the centred root-frequency statement; L11 sl. Cervical disc prolapse · Symptoms · Signs of myelopathy, which print the same. The exit-level explanation is supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
        "qs": []
