        "id": "np-5",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "The non-neurological systemic causes",
        "body": "|**Cause**|**As printed**|\n|---|---|\n|**FIBROMYALGIA**|**the most common cause of GENERALIZED MUSCULOSKELETAL PAIN in WOMEN aged 20-55**. Pain may start **localized to the neck and shoulders** and then **spread diffusely over the body**. Associated with **DEPRESSION and FATIGUE in 90 % of cases** and with **sleep disorders**. ⚠️ **Diagnostic criteria: widespread musculoskeletal pain and excess tenderness in at least 11 OF 18 predefined anatomic sites**|\n|**POLYMYALGIA RHEUMATICA**|patients **OVER 50**. **Severe aching, pain and tenderness in the NECK AND SHOULDER GIRDLE muscles**. ⚠️ **MARKEDLY ELEVATED ESR**. **Responds DRAMATICALLY to SMALL DOSES of oral STEROID**|\n|**TENDONITIS, BURSITIS and ARTHRITIS**|**pain TRIGGERED BY JOINT MOVEMENT** — shoulder, elbow, wrist or hand — suggests **tendonitis, capsulitis or an internal derangement of the joint**|\n\n- **⚠️ Three causes — three different discriminators, and none of them is the neck.** **Fibromyalgia spreads BEYOND the neck**; **polymyalgia rheumatica has a RAISED ESR and a dramatic steroid response**; **the joint disorders hurt ON MOVEMENT of a joint, not the neck.** **A patient whose neck pain is not made worse by moving the neck is on this page.**\n- **⚠️ Age separates the first two:** fibromyalgia **20-55 and female**; polymyalgia rheumatica **over 50**. **They overlap only in a narrow band, and the ESR decides it.**\n- **⚠️ The steroid response is diagnostic — as well as therapeutic** for polymyalgia rheumatica — the book calls it **dramatic**, and **small doses**, which is the detail that separates it from the higher doses giant-cell arteritis needs. *(That contrast is supplied; this chapter never mentions giant-cell arteritis.)*\n\n### ⚠️ The poster, and two defects in it\n- **The poster on 168:** it **restates all six causes** — the three neurological ones from `np-1`, `np-2` and `np-4` and the three above — **and adds no fact.**\n- **⚠️ It misprints `women` as `wornen`**, verified at 300 dpi, and **its clipboard glyph renders the `11` of *11 of 18* as an illegible squiggle**. **The prose on the same page carries both correctly.**\n- **⚠️ It renames `Tendonitis — Bursitis, and Arthritis` as `Inflammatory Joint Disorders`** — a label that **appears nowhere in the prose**. **Recorded, not corrected.**\n- **⚠️ And it loosens one fact:** the poster says cervical disc prolapse is **typically at C5-C7**, where the prose says **C5-C6 and C6-C7**. **Compatible, but the poster is the vaguer statement.**\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed p.168 — `Non-Neurological Systemic Causes:` and the unnumbered six-cause poster, re-read at 300 dpi to confirm the misprint and the illegible numeral. The giant-cell arteritis contrast is supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
