        "body": "### ⚠️ The deck asks two MCQs and prints no answer\n|**Question**|**Options as printed**|**⚠️ The answer, worked from the sources**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**1. What is the most common cause of neck pain?**|A. cervical spondylosis · B. acute cervical disc prolapse · C. ankylosing spondylitis · D. Pott's disease of the spine|**A — CERVICAL SPONDYLOSIS.** Both sources print the words *the most common cause* against it (`np-1`). ⚠️ **Neither ankylosing spondylitis nor Pott's disease appears anywhere in this chapter** — they are distractors the material does not cover|\n|**2. A 50-year-old presents with numbness in the THUMB. Most probable root?**|A. C5 · B. C6 · C. C7 · D. C8|**B — C6.** The cervical root table gives **C6 sensory = THUMB** (`np-3`); C5 is the lateral arm, C7 the middle finger, C8 the little finger. ⚠️ **All four options are rows of one table** — this question is that table and nothing else|\n\n- **⚠️ Worked, not supplied:** both answers come from **the chapter's own printed material**, not from outside — but **the deck prints neither**, so they stand as workings and not as the lecturer's key.\n\n### ⚠️ Register (§14.5) — filed IN, and paid\n- **`nr-backpain`'s `bp-9` filed SIX rows here — and all six are paid:** **neck pain and brachialgia with its epidemiology** and **cervical spondylosis** (`np-1`) · **cervical disc prolapse** (`np-2`) · **ulnar entrapment** (`np-4`) · **non-neurological systemic causes, including fibromyalgia** (`np-5`) · **the cervical root table** (`np-3`) · **the twelve specific tests** (`np-6`) · **investigations and treatment of neck pain** (`np-7`).\n- **No row is filed OUT of this chapter.** ✅ **Book Neurology ch.16 is now FULLY SPENT** across `nr-backpain` and `nr-neckpain` — **all sixteen printed pages, 157 to 172.**\n\n### ⚠️ Three things this half contradicts, records or leaves open\n|**Point**|**State**|\n|---|---|\n|**MRI**|**unscoped here** (`np-7`), **scoped to post-discectomy recurrence** in the lumbar half (`bp-5`). **Both recorded, neither reconciled**|\n|**C5/C6 motor**|**the table and the panel disagree** — the panel gives biceps to both roots, the table gives C5 deltoid only (`np-3`)|\n|**The poster's defects**|**`wornen` for `women`**, an **illegible `11`**, a **renamed category**, and a **looser disc level** (`np-5`) — **all four recorded, none corrected**|\n\n### What this half does not contain\n- **⚠️ No T1 column in the root table**, although the figure beneath it teaches T1 and `np-1` names a T1 sign.\n- **⚠️ `RA & MT` are never expanded**, and **no drug or dose is printed** for the analgesics and anti-inflammatories in `np-7`.\n- **⚠️ Cervical spondylosis:** it has **no investigation or treatment of its own** — the only numbered topic in the chapter without a sub-structure (`np-1`).\n- **⚠️ `Post-Operative Physiotherapy`:** a **heading with no body**, and the chapter's last line.\n- **No whiplash and no thoracic outlet syndrome**; **cervical rib** is named only as what **Adson's test is for** (`np-6`); and **no giant-cell arteritis**, despite polymyalgia rheumatica being taught in `np-5`.\n- **⚠️ The pain taxonomy:** absent from **BOTH halves** — the verified absence and the orphan question `npqb-nr-125` that depends on it are recorded in **`nr-backpain`'s `bp-9`**.\n\nSrc: L11 sl. Question 1 · Question 2, which print the stems and options and no key; the answers are worked from printed pp. 166 and 169 of neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16. The register is audited against `nr-backpain`'s `bp-9`; the cache is content\\neuro\\book\\neuro-ch16-pain.txt",
