        "h": "Serotonin syndrome, and the organic mimics",
        "body": "### Serotonin syndrome\n- **Mechanism:** **OVERACTIVATION of 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors.**\n- **Iatrogenic triggers:** **SSRIs + MAOIs** · **TRAMADOL or FENTANYL** · **DEXTROMETHORPHAN.**\n- **Diagnosis:** the **HUNTER TOXICITY CRITERIA must be met** for a definitive diagnosis in the acute setting — **neuromuscular:** **spontaneous or INDUCIBLE CLONUS**, **tremor**; **autonomic:** **HYPERREFLEXIA**, **agitation**, **diaphoresis**; **specific:** **HYPERTONIA and HYPERTHERMIA > 38 °C PLUS CLONUS.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ IT PAYS `ph-5`: an OPEN GAP in `ps-pharm`**, which records that **neither of its two sources names a single precipitant drug pair, nor cyproheptadine, nor any treatment beyond *proper medical intervention*.** `L16` names **all three**. **`ph-5` keeps the SYMPTOM LISTS and the 24-hour window; this section keeps the MECHANISM, the TRIGGERS, the CRITERIA and the ANTIDOTE.** **Neither repeats the other.**\n- **⚠️ Two ordinary prescriptions: TRAMADOL is `ps-sud`'s class-6 drug of abuse** and **an SSRI is `ps-pharm`'s first-line antidepressant.** **This is the emergency that the two ordinary prescriptions make together.**\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ NMS against serotonin syndrome — the chapter's set-piece comparison\n|**Feature**|**NMS**|**SEROTONIN SYNDROME**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Trigger**|**DOPAMINE ANTAGONISTS**|**serotonergic overstimulation** (SSRIs/MAOIs)|\n|**⚠️ ONSET**|**SUBACUTE — days to weeks**|**HYPERACUTE — minutes to 24 h**|\n|**⚠️ MUSCLE**|**severe LEAD-PIPE RIGIDITY**, **BRADYKINESIA**|**HYPERREFLEXIA, CLONUS, distinct TREMORS**|\n|**Autonomic**|**labile blood pressure**, **profuse diaphoresis**|**GI hyperactivity**, **MYDRIASIS**|\n|**⚠️ ANTIDOTE**|**BROMOCRIPTINE / DANTROLENE**|**CYPROHEPTADINE**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ Two discriminators: ONSET and MUSCLE TONE.** **Slow and stiff is NMS; fast and jerky is serotonin syndrome** — and **the antidotes are opposite**, so the distinction decides treatment. **Cyproheptadine is named nowhere else in the module.**\n\n### Medical mimics — misstep 1, itemised\n|**Group**|**Entity, and how it presents**|\n|---|---|\n|**Autoimmune**|**ANTI-NMDA RECEPTOR ANTIBODIES** — **acute psychosis and memory deficits in YOUNG FEMALES.** ⚠️ **Clara's diagnosis** (`em-1`)|\n|**Paraneoplastic**|**LIMBIC ENCEPHALITIS**, triggered by **occult LUNG or TESTICULAR malignancy**|\n|**Endocrine**|**THYROID STORM** — **frank psychosis, emotional lability, tachycardia**|\n|**Metabolic**|**DKA and SEPSIS** — **profound disorientation and combativeness**|\n|**⚠️ AIP**|**ACUTE INTERMITTENT PORPHYRIA** — **abdominal pain + acute psychosis/hallucinations + DARK URINE**|\n|**Nutritional**|**WERNICKE ENCEPHALOPATHY** — **ophthalmoparesis, ataxia, mental status change**, from **THIAMINE deficiency**|\n|**Genetic**|**WILSON DISEASE** — **copper overload**; ⚠️ **psychiatric symptoms often PRECEDE the tremor**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ THE RED-FLAG TRIAD: ACUTE ONSET + CONFUSION + AUTONOMIC SHIFTS.** `L16` prints it as the rule for when to stop assuming a primary psychiatric illness. **Never assume primary psychosis; screen for the mimics.**\n\nSrc: L16 sl. Overstimulation of Serotonin · The Hunter Serotonin Toxicity Criteria · NMS vs. Serotonin Syndrome · Medical Mimics: Organic Pathologies · Mimics: Endocrine & Metabolic · Mimics: Nutritional & Cognitive · Guarding Against Missed Emergencies. ⚠️ **`L15` teaches none of this section**",
