        "h": "Where it came from — Freud, hypnosis, free association",
        "body": "### The line of descent the deck draws\n- **Psychoanalysis:** the origin of psychotherapy, **in the work of the Viennese neurologist SIGMUND FREUD**, who had **studied under the French neurologist JEAN-MARTIN CHARCOT.**\n- **The starting technique — HYPNOSIS:** Freud first used **the well-known techniques of hypnosis** to treat patients suffering from **what was then called HYSTERICAL PARALYSIS.**\n- **The observation, with JOSEF BREUER:** their patients **tended to RELIVE EARLIER LIFE EXPERIENCES** that could be **associated with the symptomatic expression of their illnesses.**\n- **The finding:** when **those memories and the emotions attached to them were brought to consciousness during the hypnotic state**, the patients **showed improvement.**\n- **The step that made a method of it:** most patients proved **able to talk about such memories WITHOUT being under hypnosis.**\n- **FREE ASSOCIATION —** the production by the patient, **aloud and without suppression or self-censorship of any kind**, of **the thoughts and feelings uppermost in their mind.** It is Freud's **means of access to the unconscious**, and **from it psychoanalysis gradually developed.**\n\n- **⚠️ This is the module's only history.** `psychoanaly` **returns exactly one hit across all 25 cached decks — this one.** Nowhere else is a psychiatric treatment given an origin story, and nowhere else are Charcot or Breuer named.\n\nSrc: L19 sl. Development of Psychotherapy ×2 (the portrait slide and the prose slide), both in full",
