        "h": "The six types the lecture teaches",
        "body": "### The deck's own six\n*One slide each, and this table is all six in full.*\n\n|**Type**|**What it holds and what it is for**|\n|---|---|\n|**PSYCHODYNAMIC psychotherapy**|behaviour and mental well-being are **influenced by CHILDHOOD EXPERIENCES** and by **inappropriate repetitive thoughts or feelings that are UNCONSCIOUS** (outside awareness). Work with the therapist **improves SELF-AWARENESS** and **changes old patterns**, so the person can **more fully take charge of their life**. ⚠️ **PSYCHOANALYSIS is a MORE INTENSIVE FORM of it — sessions THREE OR MORE TIMES A WEEK**|\n|**COGNITIVE BEHAVIORAL therapy (CBT)**|helps people **identify and change thinking and behaviour patterns that are HARMFUL or INEFFECTIVE**, replacing them with **more accurate thoughts and functional behaviours**. **Focuses on CURRENT problems and how to solve them**, and **often involves practising new skills in the real world**. For **depression, anxiety, trauma-related disorders and eating disorders**|\n|**DIALECTICAL BEHAVIORAL therapy (DBT)**|**a SPECIFIC TYPE OF CBT that helps REGULATE EMOTIONS.** Often used for **chronic suicidal thoughts, BORDERLINE PERSONALITY DISORDER, eating disorders and PTSD**. Teaches **new skills** so people **take personal responsibility to change unhealthy or disruptive behaviour**. ⚠️ **It involves BOTH individual AND group therapy**|\n|**INTERPERSONAL therapy (IPT)**|**a SHORT-TERM STRUCTURED psychotherapy.** Helps patients understand **troublesome underlying interpersonal issues** — **unresolved GRIEF**, **changes in SOCIAL or WORK ROLES**, **CONFLICTS with significant others**, **problems RELATING to others**. Teaches **healthy ways to express emotions** and **improves communication**. **Most often used to treat DEPRESSION**|\n|**HUMANISTIC therapy**|emphasises **being your TRUE SELF in order to lead the most fulfilling life**. Core belief: **people are GOOD AT HEART and can make the right choices for themselves**; **if people do not hold themselves in high regard it is harder to develop their full potential**. ⚠️ **CARL ROGERS developed CLIENT-CENTRED THERAPY, the MAIN TYPE of humanistic therapy**|\n|**SUPPORTIVE therapy**|uses **GUIDANCE and ENCOURAGEMENT** to help patients **develop their OWN resources**. Builds **self-esteem**, **reduces anxiety**, **STRENGTHENS COPING MECHANISMS**, and **improves social and community functioning**. Helps patients **deal with the issues their mental health conditions raise across the rest of their lives**|\n\n### ⚠️ Three more types the module teaches, which this list does not contain\n- **COMPLIANCE ENHANCEMENT therapy —** matched to **bipolar disorders** on L9,10's psychotherapy slide (`ps-mood`). **L19 never names it.**\n- **COGNITIVE REMEDIATION —** in L11,12's psychotherapy column for schizophrenia (`ps-schizmgmt`). **One hit across the 25 decks.**\n- **MILIEU therapy —** same column; and the **book** lists it among seven psychosocial interventions (`th-6`).\n\n- **⚠️ One slide of the six cannot be read:** the slide **after CBT** carries **a 1275x1651 portrait figure and three words of text** — almost certainly the CBT thought-behaviour-emotion diagram, but **that is an inference and it is not written into this chapter.** A render needs a subagent; **recorded in `th-11`.**\n- **⚠️ So is the ADDITIONAL THERAPIES slide:** *Additional therapies sometimes used in combination with psychotherapy include* — **and the list that follows it is THREE IMAGES.** **The deck promises a list and prints a picture of one.**\n\nSrc: L19 sl. Types of therapy (the six-panel figure) · Psychodynamic psychotherapy · Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) · Dialectical behavioral therapy (DBT) · Interpersonal therapy (IPT) · Humanistic therapy · Supportive therapy — all six in full. Unreadable pages measured with pdffonts and pdfimages, not assumed. Three extra types: L9,10 · L11,12 caches",
