        "body": "### Current evidence-based recommendations — the NICE slide in full\n|**Therapy**|**Recommended for**|\n|---|---|\n|**CBT**|**psychosis/schizophrenia · depression · anxiety disorders · eating disorders · PTSD**|\n|**Interpersonal therapy (IPT)**|**depression · eating disorders**|\n|**MBT**|**personality disorder**|\n|**MBCT**|**RELAPSE PREVENTION in depression**|\n|**DBT**|**personality disorder**|\n|**CAT**|**depression · anxiety disorders · personality disorders**|\n|**Psychodynamic psychotherapy**|**depression · anxiety disorders · PTSD · personality disorder**|\n|**Behavioural psychotherapy**|**ADDICTION disorders**|\n|**Family/systemic therapy**|**eating disorders · psychosis/schizophrenia**|\n|**Counselling and supportive psychotherapy**|**ACCORDING TO PATIENT PREFERENCE**|\n\n- **⚠️ Three acronyms are never expanded —** not here and not anywhere in the module. **MBT** is *mentalization-based therapy*, **MBCT** *mindfulness-based cognitive therapy*, **CAT** *cognitive analytic therapy* — **expansions supplied, not taken from the course material**; **what each one does is not supplied, because the deck gives no basis for it.**\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ THE DECK AND THE BOOK CONTRADICT EACH OTHER ON CBT IN SCHIZOPHRENIA\n|**Source**|**What it says**|\n|---|---|\n|**L19**, NICE slide|**CBT for people with PSYCHOSIS/SCHIZOPHRENIA** — first row of the table above|\n|**Book Psych ch.7**, printed p.238|*Evidence for **CBT** in either **reducing symptoms** or **preventing relapse** is **MINIMAL**.* And **art/drama therapy are NOT WELL-RESEARCHED**|\n\n- **⚠️ Both recorded, NEITHER corrected.** They are **two sources of equal standing disagreeing on a recommendation**, not a transcription fault — the same handling `ps-pharm` gave the dopamine pathways. **They agree on family/systemic therapy for schizophrenia**, which both list.\n\n### What the rest of the module matches to what\n|**Disorder**|**Therapy, and where it is taught**|\n|---|---|\n|**Major depressive disorder**|**CBT** — L9,10, `ps-mood`|\n|**Adjustment disorder, depressive type**|**SUPPORTIVE psychotherapy** — L9,10, `ps-mood`|\n|**Bipolar disorders**|**COMPLIANCE ENHANCEMENT therapy** — L9,10, `ps-mood`|\n|**GAD**|**CBT** — and in the same box **ECT is NOT EFFECTIVE** and **hospitalization is usually not needed** (L4,5, `ps-anxiety`)|\n|**OCD**|**CBT-ERP ± SSRI, FIRST LINE**, stepped care per NICE 2005 updated 2021 — L8, `ps-ocd`|\n|**Schizophrenia**|**milieu · family · CBT · cognitive remediation** — L11,12, `ps-schizmgmt`|\n|**Somatization disorder**|**GROUP psychotherapy** — social support and relief of anxiety, L3, `ps-somato`|\n|**Primary dementia**|**individual and group therapy**, with cognitive enhancement and environmental modification — L13, `ps-geri`|\n|**Substance use disorder**|**CBT · motivational interviewing · the 12-steps model · family therapy** — L15, `ps-sud`, **all four named and none described**|\n\n### What the book adds, from two chapters already spent\n- **Anxiety and related disorders —** psychotherapy is the **MAIN LINE OF TREATMENT FOR MILD CASES**; in **moderate and severe cases a COMBINATION of psychopharmacological and psychotherapeutic intervention yields BETTER RESULTS THAN PHARMACOTHERAPY ALONE.** Its four **common methods** are **relaxation techniques · systematic desensitization · thought stopping · exposure and response prevention.**\n- **Schizophrenia —** **seven** psychosocial interventions are named: **family therapy · milieu therapy · supported employment · skills training · token economic interventions · substance use · weight management.** ⚠️ **Not one is described** — `ps-schizmgmt` recorded that, and this chapter cannot repair it either.\n- **⚠️ The one head-to-head comparison:** the module prints exactly one, L8's evidence line — **ERP achieves 50-70 % symptom reduction, and meta-analyses confirm its SUPERIORITY OVER SUPPORTIVE THERAPY and waitlist.** *(Foa et al., 2005; APA Guidelines 2007, as printed.)*\n\nSrc: L19 sl. Current Evidence-based Recommendations for Psychotherapies, all ten rows verbatim. Book: neuropsychiatry.pdf Psych ch.3 printed p.200 · Psych ch.7 printed p.238, both from the existing caches — no page re-read. Matching table: L3, L4,5, L8, L9,10, L11,12, L13, L15 caches",
