        "h": "Basic counselling skills — the twelve",
        "body": "### What counselling skills are\n- **Counselling skills:** the **SOFT (interpersonal) and HARD (technical) attributes** a counsellor **puts to use in order to best help their clients work through personal issues** and **overcome obstacles that are currently preventing them from living a full and happy life.**\n- **The core of the thing —** *building a **THERAPEUTIC RAPPORT** is at the core of therapy and counselling.* ⚠️ **`rapport`, like `therapeutic alliance` in `th-7`, is a ONE-HIT term across the 25 decks** — and both of the deck's claims about what makes therapy work are about **the relationship**, not the technique.\n\n### ⚠️ Twelve skills over two slides, and NOT ONE IS DEFINED\n*The names are the deck's. **The right-hand column is supplied and is NOT taken from the course material** — it is what each term means in ordinary counselling practice, so that a bare list becomes usable.*\n\n|**Skill, as printed**|**What it is**|\n|---|---|\n|**Active listening**|attending to **what is said and what is not**, without interrupting and without rehearsing a reply|\n|**Reflecting and paraphrasing**|giving the client's own account **back to them, condensed**, so the meaning can be checked|\n|**Summarising**|**drawing a stretch of the session together** — what has been covered, what is still open|\n|**Non-verbal communication**|**posture, eye contact, tone and silence** — what the counsellor conveys while the client is talking|\n|**Attending**|**physical and mental presence**: orienting to the client and staying oriented|\n|**Empathy**|understanding the experience **from inside the client's frame**, and **showing that you have**|\n|**Unconditioned positive regard**|**accepting the client without conditions or judgement.** ⚠️ **The deck writes *unconditioned*; the term as it is usually printed is *UNCONDITIONAL positive regard*** — Carl Rogers', and the humanistic thread of `th-5`. **Recorded as printed, not silently corrected**|\n|**Open ended questions**|questions that **cannot be answered yes or no**, so the client chooses the direction|\n|**Clarifying and the use of questions**|**checking an ambiguity** instead of assuming a meaning|\n|**Interpretation**|offering **a meaning the client has not yet put into words** — the psychodynamic thread of `th-5`|\n|**Focusing**|**narrowing a diffuse account** to the one thread worth working on|\n|**Self awareness**|the counsellor's grip on **their own reactions**, so those are not mistaken for the client's|\n\n### ⚠️ How the deck spells it\n- **Three spellings in five slides:** the section title reads **Basic *Councelling* skills**, the next slide **What are *counseling* skills?**, and the three list slides **Counselling skills.** **Cited as printed** — a source's spelling is recorded, never quietly repaired.\n- **The last slide is *Counselling skills — Role play*, and it is a photograph.** **No role-play scenario, no script, no marking scheme is printed.**\n\nSrc: L19 sl. Basic Councelling skills · What are counseling skills? · Counselling skills ×2 (all twelve names verbatim) · Counselling skills — Role play (photograph). Definitions supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
