        "h": "Motor examination — tone, and the six grades of power",
        "body": "### Tone — and the two hypertonias are the examinable pair\n|**Hypertonia**|**Resistance**|**Lesion**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**SPASTICITY**|**CLASP-KNIFE** — high, then giving way|**PYRAMIDAL / motor cortex**|\n|**RIGIDITY**|**UNIFORM** throughout (lead-pipe), or **ON-AND-OFF** (cog-wheel)|**EXTRAPYRAMIDAL / BASAL GANGLIA**|\n\n- **⚠️ A FOUR-PANEL figure, p.18:** its panels are labelled **`little resistance`** · **`uniform resistance`** · **`on-and-off resistance`** (drawn with a cog-wheel graphic) · **`high to low resistance`** (a jagged arrow). **The four panels are the four tone states; the right-hand column above pairs them with the lesion.**\n- **This is what `npqb-nr-29` turns on:** **spasticity → motor cortex**, **rigidity → basal ganglia.**\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ POWER — the six grades, and the source's typesetting is broken\n|**Grade**|**As printed**|\n|---|---|\n|**0**|**No movement at all**|\n|**1**|**FLICKERS** — movement too fine to move a joint|\n|**2**|**Can move WITH ELIMINATION OF GRAVITY** (parallel to the floor)|\n|**3**|**Can move AGAINST GRAVITY, but NOT against resistance**|\n|**4**|**Can overcome MILD resistance**|\n|**5**|**NORMAL power** — can oppose any resistance|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ ON THE PAGE — GRADE 3 HAS NO BULLET OF ITS OWN.** Its text **runs on to the end of the grade-2 bullet**, so the printed list looks like five items. **All six grades 0-5 are present and nothing is missing — the typesetting is broken, not the content.** Verified at 150 dpi against the render. **Recorded, not silently repaired.**\n- **⚠️ The book never writes `MRC`.** The grading is printed under the bare heading **`Degree of weakness`**. *A reader who has met it as the MRC scale should know the book does not use the name.*\n\n### How power is tested, and what to report\n- **Examine BOTH sides:** groups of muscles around each joint, comparing left with right.\n- **Free movement FIRST, then resistance** — e.g. for elbow flexors, ask the patient to flex, then try to extend.\n- **Report three things:** *whether there is weakness · its DEGREE · and whether there is a PATTERN.*\n\n### Patterns of weakness\n- **HEMIPARESIS:** weakness of **one side of the body.** · **PARAPARESIS:** **both lower limbs.** · **MONOPARESIS:** **one limb.**\n- **PROXIMAL vs DISTAL** — and the book's rule: **LMN weakness is usually BILATERAL AND SYMMETRICAL — PROXIMAL in MYOPATHIES, DISTAL in NEUROPATHIES.**\n- **⚠️ UMN weakness is PYRAMIDAL:** **unilateral, opposite the lesion**, and **specific muscle groups go first — in the UPPER limb the ELBOW EXTENSORS are weaker than the flexors; in the LOWER limb the KNEE FLEXORS are weaker than the extensors.**\n\nSrc: Book ch.2 printed p.18 (tone, the four-panel figure) · p.19 (Degree of weakness verbatim, incl. the layout fault; Patterns of weakness) · ch.3 printed p.37 (the UMN/LMN distribution rules)",
