        "h": "Reflexes — roots, grades, and the two superficial ones that localise",
        "body": "### ⚠️⚠️ THE FIVE DEEP TENDON REFLEXES AND THEIR ROOTS\n|**Biceps**|**Brachioradialis**|**Triceps**|**Knee**|**Ankle**|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**C5, 6**|**C5, 6**|**C6, 7**|**L3, 4**|**S1, 2**|\n\n- **⚠️ Read the roots as a LEVEL TEST — which is what the questions ask.** A lesion **AT** the reflex's own root **abolishes** it — that is an LMN sign. A lesion **ABOVE** it **exaggerates** it — that is UMN. **So a tumour compressing C4 leaves the triceps arc (C6,7) intact but cuts the pyramidal supply to it, and the triceps reflex is EXAGGERATED.**\n- *The book prints the roots inside a five-panel figure, each panel drawing the manoeuvre. Its own labels read `BICEPS R.`, `BRACIORADIALIS R.` — the book's misspelling — and `TRICEPS R.`; the knee and ankle panels are unlabelled.*\n\n### Technique, and the grading\n- **A good reflex =** good limb positioning **+** localizing the tendon **+** good hammer tapping **±** **REINFORCEMENT when needed.**\n- **Reinforcement:** **upper limb** — **clench teeth**; **lower limb** — **clench the teeth, or lock fingers and pull one against the other.**\n\n|**Grade**|**Means**|\n|---|---|\n|**zero**|**ABSENT after reinforcement**|\n|**0+**|**PRESENT after reinforcement**|\n|**1**|**just elicited**|\n|**2**|**brisk**|\n|**3**|**EXAGGERATED**|\n|**4**|**EXAGGERATED WITH CLONUS**|\n\n- **⚠️ Grade 3 against 4 — CLONUS:** that single word is the whole distinction, and it is what `npqb-nr-21` tests.\n- **CLONUS:** *a repetitive contraction in response to sudden sustained contraction.* **PATELLAR** — suddenly push the patella downwards in an extended limb; if positive it **moves up and down.** **ANKLE** — flex the knee and ankle, then suddenly contract the foot; if positive the foot **repeatedly flexes and extends.**\n\n### Why a reflex is brisk or absent\n|**HYPER-reflexia**|**HYPO-reflexia / areflexia**|\n|---|---|\n|**ANXIETY**|**congenital areflexia**|\n|**PYRAMIDAL LESION ABOVE THE CENTRE OF THE REFLEX**|**UMN lesion IN SHOCK STAGE**|\n|**THYROTOXICOSIS**|**LMN lesions** — AHC, root lesions, polyneuropathy — and **HYPOTHYROIDISM**|\n\n- **⚠️ Two of the three are NOT neurological:** anxiety and thyrotoxicosis. **A brisk reflex is not by itself a pyramidal sign**, which is why `npqb-nr-8` keys *all* of them.\n- **⚠️ And the trap in the right-hand column: an acute UMN lesion is AREFLEXIC**, not hyperreflexic — *spinal shock, written in full in `nr-hemi` `hem-9`.*\n\n### The two superficial reflexes that localise\n|**Reflex**|**Roots**|**Normal**|**Abnormal means**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**ABDOMINAL**|**upper T7-T10 · lower T10-T12**|**PRESENT** — strike the relaxed abdomen, knees slightly flexed, **from outside inwards**, and compare sides|**⚠️ ABSENT = an UPPER MOTOR NEURON LESION ON THAT SIDE**|\n|**PLANTAR**|**S1**|**the BIG TOE FLEXES** — strike the **outer side** of the foot with a key, from heel towards the toes|**EXTENSION — dorsiflexion ± FANNING of the other toes = BABINSKI'S SIGN**|\n\n- **⚠️ Extensor plantar — FOUR causes**, and only one is a lesion: **1. PYRAMIDAL TRACT LESION · 2. NORMAL IN BABIES UNDER 12 MONTHS · 3. DEEP COMA · 4. DURING SEIZURES AND POST-ICTALLY.**\n- **The book also lists VISCERAL reflexes** — **vesical** (urinary flow and bladder), **genital** (ask about potency), **anal** (defecation and sphincters).\n- **⚠️ FOUR PATHOLOGICAL REFLEXES, bare:** **Glabellar · Wattenberg · Finger jerk · Hoffman.** **No method, no normal response, no interpretation for any of the four.** *The list is bare in the source; nothing was lost in transcription.*\n\nSrc: Book ch.2 printed p.21 (roots, technique, reinforcement, clonus, all verbatim) · p.22 (grades, the hyper/hypo table, abdominal and plantar in full) · p.23 (visceral and pathological reflexes)",
