        "body": "### How the book examines sensation\n- **Order:** **DISTAL to PROXIMAL**, and **start from the PATHOLOGICAL side and move to the normal side.**\n- **Three superficial modalities:** **PAIN · TOUCH · TEMPERATURE.** *Pain is tested with a **disposable pin** — a toothpick will do — pricking from an area of normal sensation.* **⚠️ Pain is carried by the LATERAL SPINOTHALAMIC TRACT**, which is the sentence that makes the cord table below work.\n- **Deep:** **VIBRATION** — a **128 Hz** tuning fork on the medial malleolus · **JOINT POSITION** · **PRESSURE.** *(⚠️ On the instrument list, printed p.9, the book writes `Tuning fork 128` with **no unit**; the unit appears only here, printed p.24. Both as printed.)*\n- **Cortical:** **point localization · two-point discrimination · graphesthesia · STEREOGNOSIS.**\n- **⚠️ The stated GOAL of deep sensory testing** is *to differentiate between **POLYNEUROPATHY** and sensory affection due to **CORD LESION**.*\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ THE EIGHT PATTERNS OF SENSORY LOSS — printed p.23, a figure and nothing else\n|**Pattern**|**Distribution**|\n|---|---|\n|**A generalised peripheral neuropathy**|**GLOVE AND STOCKING** — both hands, both feet and lower legs|\n|**B SENSORY ROOTS**|**BANDS** — the figure marks **C5, C7, L5** on the limbs|\n|**C single DORSAL COLUMN lesion**|one leg and the lower trunk|\n|**D TRANSVERSE thoracic cord lesion**|**everything below a mid-trunk SENSORY LEVEL**|\n|**E UNILATERAL cord lesion (BROWN-SÉQUARD)**|**two different shades on OPPOSITE lower limbs** — the dissociated loss|\n|**F CENTRAL cord lesion**|a **CAPE / SHAWL** over both shoulders, upper trunk and arms|\n|**G MID-BRAINSTEM lesion**|**one half of the FACE plus the OPPOSITE half of the BODY**|\n|**H HEMISPHERE (THALAMIC) lesion**|**one whole half of the body, INCLUDING the face**|\n\n- **⚠️ Panel B is the answer to `npqb-nr-111`: SEGMENTAL sensory loss is ROOT sensory loss** — a band, not a stocking and not a level. **Root → band · nerve → glove and stocking · cord → level · thalamus → whole half including face.**\n- *The book writes the stocking pattern `stoking` on printed 23 and `stalking` on printed 26. Both as printed.*\n\n### ⚠️ The cord by column — and the book's vocabulary is not the question's\n|**Column**|**What runs in it**|**A lesion gives**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**DORSAL (posterior) column**|**deep sensation only** — gracile and cuneate|**PURE SENSORY deficit** — no weakness|\n|**LATERAL column**|**⚠️ the book NEVER uses this phrase.** By its own Brown-Séquard figure the lateral half carries the **`Corticospinal tract`** and the **`Spinothalamic tract`**|**A MIXTURE — MOTOR plus SUPERFICIAL SENSORY**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ ROW TWO IS COMPOSED, NOT QUOTED.** The book labels both tracts on the cord section and states elsewhere that the **corticospinal tract is motor** and the **lateral spinothalamic carries pain**; **the conclusion follows from its own labels, but the phrase `lateral column` is the question bank's, not the book's.** *Recorded so the reasoning is visible rather than implied.*\n\n### Transection and hemisection — the four cases, as printed\n|**Lesion**|**Result**|\n|---|---|\n|**COMPLETE transection ABOVE C5**|loss of **all ascending sensation from both sides** + loss of motor function in **all four limbs** — **QUADRIPLEGIA**|\n|**HEMI-section ABOVE C5**|**motor loss on the SAME side** (spinal hemiplegia) · **DEEP sensation lost on the SAME side** · **SUPERFICIAL sensation lost on the OPPOSITE side** — **BROWN-SÉQUARD**|\n|**COMPLETE transection BELOW T1, above L2**|loss of all sensation from both sides **up to a SENSORY LEVEL** + **PARAPLEGIA**|\n|**HEMI-section BELOW T1, above L2**|**MONOPLEGIA** of the lower limb on the same side · deep sensation lost same side · superficial lost opposite side, **up to the level of the hemi-section**|\n\n- **⚠️ `npqb-nr-34` is row two exactly: IPSILATERAL deep sensory and motor, CONTRALATERAL superficial.** The reason is in `nr-intro` `in-5` — **the dorsal columns are uncrossed in the cord, the spinothalamic tracts are crossed.**\n- **⚠️ Two source faults recorded here.** The chapter says **QUADRIPLEGIA** on printed 38 where **its own figure on printed 39 says TETRAPLEGIA**; and the figure title prints **Brown-Séquard** with the accent while the text prints **Brown-Sequard** without it. **Both as printed.**\n- **⚠️ And one gap the book leaves open:** item b) **does not state the level at which the contralateral superficial loss begins**, and **never mentions the ipsilateral band of anaesthesia at the level of the lesion.** *Not covered by the chapter; not supplied here.*\n- *The full spinal syndromes, the sensory level and the compression signs are `nr-hemi` `hem-6`/`hem-8` and `nr-backpain`. Not repeated.*\n\nSrc: Book ch.2 printed pp.23-26 (modalities, the goal, the eight-panel patterns figure, all as printed) · p.24 (128 Hz) · p.9 (the unitless instrument entry). Ch.3 printed p.38 (all four transection/hemisection cases verbatim, and the Brown-Séquard figure labels) · p.39 (the tetraplegia figure)",
