        "h": "The two words, the taxonomy, and what the session is for",
        "body": "### The two words, and the one that covers both\n- **Meningitis and encephalitis:** both are **DIFFUSE INFECTIONS OF THE CNS.**\n- **MENINGITIS —** implies **PRIMARY INVOLVEMENT OF THE MENINGES.**\n- **ENCEPHALITIS —** indicates **BRAIN PARENCHYMAL INVOLVEMENT.**\n- **⚠️ MENINGOENCEPHALITIS —** because **these anatomic boundaries are often NOT DISTINCT**, many patients have evidence of **BOTH meningeal AND parenchymal involvement** and **should be considered to have meningoencephalitis.**\n\n### The deck's own taxonomy — three classes, five entities\n|**Class**|**What the deck names under it**|**Taught?**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**BACTERIAL**|**Bacterial meningitis** · **Tuberculosis**|✅ both, in full|\n|**VIRAL**|**Viral meningitis** · **Herpes simplex encephalitis**|⚠️ **HSV in full; VIRAL MENINGITIS is NAMED and never given a slide** — the nearest the lecture comes is **aseptic meningitis** (`ci-8`)|\n|**FUNGAL**|**Cryptococcal meningitis**|❌ **NAMED ONCE AND NEVER TAUGHT** — `cryptococc` returns **one hit across all 25 cached decks**, this slide|\n\n### And the meningitis list it does teach\n- **1 — ACUTE BACTERIAL MENINGITIS.**\n- **2 — TUBERCULOUS MENINGITIS.**\n- **3 — ASEPTIC MENINGITIS.**\n\n### What the session is for\n- **Detect meningitis EARLY.**\n- **DIFFERENTIATE** between the different types of meningitis.\n- **INVESTIGATE** a case of meningitis and encephalitis.\n- **Complications:** know how to **TREAT and PREVENT SERIOUS COMPLICATIONS.**\n\nSrc: L9 sl. ILOs · CNS Infection ×3 (the definition slide and the two taxonomy slides) · Meningitis — all in full. Absence probes run over all 25 caches in content/neuro/lectures/",
