        "id": "ci-2",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Case 1 — the crescendo headache, worked",
        "body": "### The presentation\n- **28-year-old man —** emergency room, **48 HOURS of headache.**\n- **The headache:** **FRONTAL AND OCCIPITAL**, with **mild nausea**; **various analgesics WITHOUT ANY IMPROVEMENT**; **intensity has GRADUALLY INCREASED since it began**, until he could no longer tolerate it.\n- **The only other symptom:** **TIGHTNESS IN THE SHOULDERS AND NECK.**\n- **Background:** **no known medical illness · NO HISTORY OF HEAD TRAUMA.**\n\n### On examination\n|**Axis**|**Finding**|\n|---|---|\n|**Temperature**|**40 °C**|\n|**Blood pressure**|110/68 mmHg|\n|**Pulse**|**110 / min**|\n|**Conscious level**|**awake, alert, FULLY ORIENTED** — but **feels he is TAKING TOO MUCH TIME TO ANSWER** the questions|\n|**Meningeal signs**|**KERNIG POSITIVE · BRUDZINSKI POSITIVE**|\n|**Skin**|**NO rash**|\n|**Cranial nerves**|normal **except BILATERAL HORIZONTAL NYSTAGMUS**|\n|**Motor, sensory, cerebellar**|**normal**|\n|**Reflexes**|**HYPERREFLEXIA THROUGHOUT, with a BABINSKI SIGN**|\n|**CT head, no contrast**|**NORMAL**|\n|**⚠️ And the line the slide adds last**|**his headache is WORSE NOW than when he presented**|\n\n### The three questions, and what the deck's own analysis answers\n- **Diagnosis — MENINGITIS.** A **CRESCENDO headache with NAUSEA, FEVER and SLOWNESS IN RESPONDING TO QUESTIONS** should **alert the clinician to MENINGITIS OR ENCEPHALITIS**; **the NECK TIGHTNESS and the KERNIG SIGN are consistent with MENINGITIS RATHER THAN ENCEPHALITIS.**\n- **⚠️ AND THE TRIAD THAT STANDS ON ITS OWN:** it is **also suspected if the patient presents with a triad of HEADACHE, FEVER and DISTURBED LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS.**\n- **Best diagnostic next step — LUMBAR PUNCTURE AND CSF STUDY.** *The best way to determine the aetiology*, and what allows **bacterial to be distinguished from viral, and so forth.** ⚠️ **But imaging comes first — the sequence is `ci-3`.**\n- **⚠️ Next step in therapy — THE DECK ASKS AND ITS ANALYSIS NEVER ANSWERS.** From its own treatment slides: **empirical VANCOMYCIN + CEFTRIAXONE with DEXAMETHASONE, started WITHOUT waiting for culture** (`ci-6`).\n\n### The two signs, defined\n- **BRUDZINSKI sign —** **passive NECK FLEXION → HIPS AND KNEES FLEX.** And: **passive flexion of ONE lower limb → HIP AND KNEE FLEXION OF THE OTHER lower limb.**\n- **KERNIG sign —** **severe stiffness of the HAMSTRINGS causes an INABILITY TO STRAIGHTEN THE LEG when the HIP IS FLEXED TO 90 DEGREES.**\n\nSrc: L9 sl. Clinical approach to \"CNS infections\" ×3 (the case) · Case analysis · the Brudzinski / Kernig slide — all in full. The therapy answer is assembled from the deck's own Acute Bacterial Meningitis treatment slides; the case analysis prints none",
