        "h": "Acute bacterial meningitis — who gets it, and from what",
        "body": "### Epidemiology\n|**Axis**|**What the deck prints**|\n|---|---|\n|**Incidence**|**5–10 cases / 100,000 population**|\n|**Sex**|**MALES are generally more affected than females**|\n|**Season**|**WINTER, SPRING and FALL**|\n\n### ⚠️ Age is keyed to the ORGANISM, not given as one range\n|**Organism**|**Age**|\n|---|---|\n|**H. influenzae**|**between 3 MONTHS and 7 YEARS**|\n|**Pneumococci**|**children AND adults**|\n|**Meningococci**|**children and adults — ⚠️ RARELY SEEN ABOVE THE AGE OF 50**|\n\n### Causative agents\n- **⚠️ THE 80 % RULE:** **STREPTOCOCCUS PNEUMONIAE, NEISSERIA MENINGITIDIS and H. INFLUENZAE** are responsible for **80 % OF CASES.**\n- **Enterobacteria —** **E. coli · Pseudomonas · Proteus · Klebsiella.**\n- **And —** **Group B streptococci · Staph. aureus · LISTERIA MONOCYTOGENES.**\n\n### Route of entry — four, lettered in the deck\n|**A**|**UPPER RESPIRATORY TRACT — DROPLET INFECTION**|\n|---|---|\n|**B**|**HAEMATOGENOUS SPREAD**|\n|**C**|**EXTENSION FROM ADJACENT STRUCTURES —** **SINUSITIS · MASTOIDITIS · EAR INFECTION**|\n|**D**|**PENETRATING WOUNDS, or IATROGENIC during LUMBAR OR BRAIN SURGERY**|\n\n- *⚠️ Route D is the mirror of `ci-3`: the lumbar puncture that diagnoses meningitis is on the deck's own list of ways to cause it.*\n\nSrc: L9 sl. Acute Bacterial Meningitis: Epidemiology · Causative Agent / Route of entry — both in full",
