        "w": "must",
        "h": "Encephalitis, and herpes simplex encephalitis",
        "body": "### Encephalitis — the general case\n- **An INFLAMMATION OF THE BRAIN**, which is **OFTEN THE RESULT OF AN UNUSUAL COMPLICATION FROM A COMMON VIRAL INFECTION.**\n- **The commonest one:** **THE MOST COMMON VIRAL ENCEPHALITIS IS HERPES SIMPLEX ENCEPHALITIS.**\n- **Many viruses can affect the brain —** **HERPES SIMPLEX VIRUS [most common]** · **EPSTEIN–BARR VIRUS** · **CYTOMEGALOVIRUS**, and the deck's list ends open.\n- *⚠️ Not every encephalitis is infective, and **L9 never says so.** `nr-epilepsy` `ep-6` carries **AUTOIMMUNE ENCEPHALITIS — anti-NMDAR, LGI1** from L4, with its own instruction to **think of it in young patients with new atypical seizures**; `ps-emerg` `em-1` works a case of it **mimicking psychosis.***\n\n### Herpes simplex encephalitis\n- **What it is:** **THE COMMONEST AND GRAVEST FORM of acute encephalitis.**\n- **⚠️ FATAL IN 30–70 % OF CASES**, and **MOST SURVIVORS HAVE SEQUELAE.**\n- **The virus:** **HERPES SIMPLEX VIRUS TYPE 1**, most commonly.\n- **How it reaches the brain:** **LOCAL SPREAD FROM OROPHARYNGEAL INFECTION**, via the **TRIGEMINAL OR OLFACTORY NERVES** — **or possible VIRAEMIA.**\n\n### Clinical picture\n- **Acute onset:** **HEADACHE, FEVER, CONFUSION, DELIRIUM, STUPOR — and it may proceed into COMA.**\n- **⚠️ OEDEMA MAY CAUSE TEMPORAL LOBE HERNIATION**, leading to **SEVERE COMA AND DEATH.**\n- **⚠️ AND THE PART THAT MAKES IT RECOGNISABLE — MANIFESTATIONS OF TEMPORAL LOBE INSULT:**\n  - **COMPLEX PARTIAL SEIZURES.**\n  - **PSYCHOTIC OR BEHAVIOURAL CHANGES.**\n  - **OLFACTORY and/or GUSTATORY HALLUCINATION.**\n  - **DYSPHASIA.**\n  - **HEMIPLEGIA.**\n- **MEMORY CHANGE — usually a LATE manifestation.**\n\n### Investigations — three, and each carries a signature\n|**Test**|**What it shows**|\n|---|---|\n|**CSF**|**increased pressure · increased protein · increased cells, MAINLY LYMPHOCYTES · NORMAL SUGAR.** ⚠️ **HSV DNA CAN BE DETECTED IN THE CSF BY PCR ASSAY — AND THIS IS THE DIAGNOSTIC TEST OF CHOICE**|\n|**CT / MRI brain**|**characteristically the lesion is in the INFERIOR AND MEDIAL PORTION OF THE TEMPORAL LOBE**, with **SCATTERED AREAS OF HAEMORRHAGE and SURROUNDING OEDEMA**|\n|**EEG**|**PERIODIC HIGH-VOLTAGE SHARP WAVES AND SLOW-WAVE COMPLEXES IN THE TEMPORAL AREA**|\n\n### Treatment\n- **IV ACYCLOVIR (Zovirax) — 10 mg/kg every 8 HOURS, FOR 2 WEEKS.**\n- **Steroids:** **CORTICOSTEROIDS MAY BE USED FOR ACUTE OEDEMA.**\n- *⚠️ The module's only other acyclovir is L8's, where it **may be of value in the early stages of BELL'S PALSY** (`nr-cranial`). Different indication, and **L8 prints no dose at all** — this is the one place in the module where an antiviral is quantified.*\n\nSrc: L9 sl. Encephalitis · Herpes Simplex Encephalitis ×5 — the aetiology slide, the two clinical-picture slides (the deck prints the picture twice), Investigations, the CT/EEG slide and Treatment — all in full. Autoimmune encephalitis: L4 via `nr-epilepsy` `ep-6`. Bell's palsy acyclovir: L8 via `nr-cranial`",
