        "h": "Case 2 — the deck asks three questions and answers none",
        "body": "### The vignette\n- **17-year-old male —** **FEVER, RECURRENT SYNCOPE AND SEIZURES FOLLOWING TOOTH EXTRACTION.**\n- Attended **a different clinic** with the syncope and seizures **the following day**; **clinical progression went worse**; transferred to hospital for advanced diagnosis.\n- **⚠️ Day three:** **went into STATUS EPILEPTICUS THREE DAYS AFTER THE EXTRACTION**, on the day of ICU admission.\n- **Medical history unremarkable.**\n- **On admission:** **fever 38.5 °C · arterial tension 125/75 mmHg · heart rate 84/min · ⚠️ KERNIG–BRUDZINSKI SIGN NEGATIVE.**\n- **Lumbar puncture:** **INSIGNIFICANT CELL COUNT · CSF GLUCOSE 72 mg/dl · PROTEIN 35 gr/dl.**\n- **Cranial MRI:** **findings concordant with LEFT TEMPORAL LOBE ENCEPHALOPATHY.**\n- **Course:** seizures **controlled with anticonvulsive and other medications**; patient **awake and cooperative**; **discharged from the ICU to the INFECTIOUS DISEASES WARD on the fourth day.**\n\n### ⚠️ The deck asks three questions and answers NONE of them\n*Worked below from the deck's own slides. The reasoning is this chapter's; every fact in it is L9's.*\n\n- **Provisional diagnosis — HERPES SIMPLEX ENCEPHALITIS.**\n\n|**Finding**|**What it does for the diagnosis**|\n|---|---|\n|**KERNIG–BRUDZINSKI NEGATIVE**|**removes the meningeal syndrome** — Case 1's discriminator run the other way: **neck signs mean meningitis rather than encephalitis, and there are none**|\n|**LEFT TEMPORAL LOBE on MRI**|**the characteristic HSV site** — inferior and medial temporal lobe (`ci-9`)|\n|**Seizures, then status epilepticus**|**COMPLEX PARTIAL SEIZURES are the first temporal-lobe manifestation the deck lists**|\n|**CSF glucose 72 mg/dl**|**NORMAL SUGAR** — the aseptic/viral column of `ci-8`, and **against both bacterial and tuberculous**|\n|**Insignificant cell count**|**consistent with a viral process**; it does not exclude HSV|\n|**Fever 38.5 °C, acute onset**|the deck's own HSV clinical picture|\n\n- **⚠️ The one thing missing:** and it is **THE DECK'S OWN TEST OF CHOICE — HSV DNA BY PCR ON THE CSF. The vignette never reports it.**\n\n### Lines of treatment\n- **IV ACYCLOVIR 10 mg/kg / 8 h for 2 WEEKS**, started **empirically on suspicion** — the mortality is 30–70 % (`ci-9`).\n- **CORTICOSTEROIDS for acute oedema.**\n- **Anticonvulsant control of the seizures**, which is the vignette's own step. *Status epilepticus is `nr-epilepsy` `ep-10` — the definition (**≥ 5 min, or ≥ 2 seizures without recovery**) and the **A–B–C → diazepam IV → phenytoin IV → ICU** protocol. Not repeated here.*\n\n### ⚠️ Two things the vignette prints and the deck never explains\n- **THE TOOTH EXTRACTION.** L9's route-of-entry list (`ci-4`) covers **extension from adjacent structures** and **haematogenous spread**, and a dental procedure fits both — **but that route belongs to bacterial meningitis, and HSV reaches the brain by trigeminal or olfactory spread.** **The deck draws no line at all from the extraction to the diagnosis.** *A dental procedure is a recognised trigger for reactivation of latent HSV in the trigeminal ganglion — which would connect the two, and is not taken from the course material.*\n- **PROTEIN \"35gr/dl\", as printed.** **35 g/dl is not a possible CSF protein.** Against `ci-6`'s own units it reads as **35 mg/dl — i.e. NORMAL**, which is what the working above uses. **⚠️ Recorded as the source prints it; a defective source is noted, never silently repaired.**\n\nSrc: L9 sl. Case 2 ( test yourself ) ×2, in full including the three unanswered questions. The working draws only on slides already cited in `ci-2` to `ci-9`",
