        "id": "so-2",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "The clinical case — the deck asks twice and answers neither",
        "body": "### The vignette\n- **40-year-old married mother** of an **11-year-old son.**\n- **Sees her GP regularly, over many years.**\n- **Complaints have VARIED —** **ABDOMINAL PAIN · DYSURIA · DYSMENORRHOEA · TIREDNESS.**\n- **Referred over the years to —** **GYNAECOLOGISTS · UROLOGISTS · GENERAL SURGEONS.**\n- **⚠️ The investigations:** **extensive, and they have revealed NO CLEAR CAUSE.**\n- **Despite this —** she **remains convinced she has a SERIOUS PHYSICAL ILLNESS THAT REMAINS UNDIAGNOSED**; she **DEMANDS FURTHER REFERRALS AND FURTHER INVESTIGATIONS.**\n- **⚠️ The outcome:** she is **DISABLED by her symptoms and UNABLE TO WORK.**\n\n### ⚠️ The deck asks two questions and answers neither\n*Worked below from the deck's own slides. **The management plan itself is `so-5` and `so-12` and is not restated here** — only what those slides mean for this patient.*\n\n- **The diagnosis:** **the deck never names it for her** — the vignette is **SOMATIC SYMPTOM (SOMATIZATION) DISORDER**, multiple somatic complaints requiring medical attention, **not associated with any physical disorder** (`so-4`). **She fits every axis**: the varied complaints, the years, the normal investigations, the **disability.** ⚠️ **The deck sets this case on slide 4 and never returns to it.**\n- **What the GP should do next — TAKE THE CARE BACK AND STOP THE REFERRAL CYCLE.**\n  - **The GP is the right doctor:** the plan makes **the primary care practitioner direct care**, and she has instead been passed between three specialties.\n  - **⚠️ Her visits are the wrong shape:** she attends **when a new symptom appears.** The plan is **scheduled visits every 4–6 weeks, kept short** — which **removes the need to produce a symptom in order to be seen.**\n  - **⚠️ Every further referral makes it worse:** referrals are used sparingly **because they add the impression that the problem is BOTH PHYSICAL AND SEVERE** — and she is **demanding more of exactly that.**\n  - **What replaces them:** **part of a physical examination at each visit**, looking for **SIGNS rather than symptoms**, and hearing the complaints as **EMOTIONAL COMMUNICATION rather than disease.**\n- **What the specialist should advise — CAUTION ABOUT HOW THE DIAGNOSIS IS GIVEN.**\n  - **⚠️ The sentence to avoid:** telling her **\"it's all in your head\" is NOT AT ALL USEFUL.**\n  - **The aim:** **ALLIANCE AND REASSURANCE**, and **not insisting she see her case as psychological at first** — **it may well take a while.**\n  - **Treatment:** **GROUP psychotherapy** for social support and relief of anxiety; **pharmacotherapy only if there is an underlying depression or anxiety.**\n\nSrc: L3 sl. Clinical case, in full including the two unanswered questions. The working draws only on slides cited in so-4, so-5 and so-12",
