        "h": "Hypochondriasis, and body dysmorphic disorder",
        "body": "### ⚠️ HYPOCHONDRIASIS — the deck gives it a heading and FIVE PICTURE SLIDES\n- **What is actually printed:** the word **`(Hypochondriasis)`**, alone on a slide, **followed by FOUR consecutive slides carrying NO TEXT AT ALL.** Measured page by page with `pdffonts` and `pdftotext`; **zero fonts means the slide is an image, not vector-outlined text, so nothing is hidden from the extractor.**\n- **⚠️ And it is a STARRED disorder:** one of the **four** the deck marks in its own classification (`so-3`) — and **its teaching content is unrecoverable from the cache.**\n- **The probe:** **`hypochond` returns hits in exactly ONE deck — this one** — and **`illness anxiety disorder` returns ZERO across all 25.** *The only other module-wide appearance is book Psychiatry ch.7, which lists hypochondriasis among the NON-SPECIFIC PRODROMAL symptoms of schizophrenia (`ps-psychosis` `sz-4`) — a different use of the word entirely.*\n- **What the term means**, so the chapter is not left with a hole: **a persistent PREOCCUPATION WITH HAVING A SERIOUS ILLNESS**, built on **misinterpretation of ordinary bodily sensations**, and **persisting DESPITE appropriate medical evaluation and reassurance.** **DSM-5 abolished the label**, redistributing its patients between **somatic symptom disorder** and a new **illness anxiety disorder** *(not taken from the course material)*.\n- **⚠️ The tag is provisional:** **book Psychiatry ch.5 covers exactly this and is UNREAD** (`so-13`). **It is the honest answer available today, not the final one.**\n\n### Body dysmorphic disorder\n- **What it is:** **PREOCCUPATION WITH AN IMAGINED DEFECT IN APPEARANCE**, or **EXCESSIVE CONCERN OVER A SLIGHT PHYSICAL ANOMALY.**\n- **Where:** the distressing preoccupation **may involve ANY PART OF THE BODY** — the deck's examples are **imagined or slight flaws of the FACE OR HEAD**: **ACNE · SCARS · THINNING HAIR · FACIAL ASYMMETRY · EXCESSIVE FACIAL HAIR.**\n- **Onset:** **often during ADOLESCENCE.**\n- **Sex:** **the male-to-female ratio is ALMOST EQUAL.**\n- **⚠️ Where these patients actually present:** **many have had consultations with SURGEONS and DERMATOLOGISTS** — **not psychiatrists.**\n- **And why they do not reach psychiatry:** **most are SECRETIVE about their symptoms and RELUCTANT TO SEEK PSYCHIATRIC TREATMENT.**\n- **⚠️ THE SURGICAL WARNING:** patients with BDD **often are POOR CANDIDATES**, because they are **UNLIKELY TO BE SATISFIED WITH THE RESULTS.**\n\n- *BDD's DSM-5 home is obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, which is what this deck's \"Moved to OCD\" slide means (`so-3`); `ps-ocd` `oc-6` gives the BDD-against-OCD discrimination, and `oc-10` records that book ch.3 names it without describing it. **This chapter is where the module actually describes it.***\n\nSrc: L3 sl. (Hypochondriasis) — the heading, and the four picture slides following it, measured not assumed · Body dysmorphic disorder ×2, both in full. Hypochondriasis definition is tagged outside knowledge; `ps-psychosis` `sz-4` for the prodromal use",
