        "id": "so-11",
        "w": "know",
        "h": "Why these patients are hard — four slides, one argument",
        "body": "### The sequence treatment usually follows\n- **An EXPENSIVE WORKUP →** **NO BIOMEDICAL DIAGNOSIS →** **WEAR AND TEAR ON THE CLINICIAN →** **FRUSTRATION FOR THE PATIENT.**\n- **⚠️ THE COST FIGURE:** treating a patient who has a somatization disorder costs **NINE TIMES** that of treating a patient who does not.\n\n### \"Sicker than the sick\"\n- **They believe they are \"sicker than the sick\"** — **despite physicians' best efforts to alleviate their symptoms.**\n- **⚠️ And it is not only belief:** they **have POORER HEALTH than those with other chronic medical conditions.**\n- **They RARELY RESPOND to treatment**, and **if they do, the response is SHORT-LIVED.**\n\n### \"A reluctance to change\"\n- **The response to advice:** **they CHALLENGE SUGGESTIONS and SOMATIZE EVERY TREATMENT.**\n- **The response to a prescription:** **they DRAMATICALLY INCREASE OR DECREASE the recommended dose ON THEIR OWN.**\n- **The consequence:** **they RARELY COMPLETE AN ADEQUATE TRIAL of any medication.**\n- **⚠️ And then they move:** **they EXHAUST THE THERAPEUTIC REPERTOIRE of one physician and MOVE ON TO THE NEXT.**\n\n### \"Caring rather than curing\"\n- **⚠️ THE PARADOX:** **the MORE the physician tries to do, the MORE symptoms the patient acquires.**\n- **What it does to the clinician:** they **may be DEMORALIZED** by the patients' **unending physical concerns**, the **inability to find a cure**, and **complaints that not enough is being done.**\n- **The conclusion the slide draws:** physicians need to realise that **further attempts to treat patients who are RELUCTANT TO CHANGE will be COUNTERPRODUCTIVE.**\n- *The slide closes with its own aphorism: **\"You can lead a horse to water, but if you try to carry that horse across, you are both going to drown.\"***\n\n### \"The wisdom of doing less\"\n- **The claim:** **sometimes DOING LESS may be the MOST EFFECTIVE STRATEGY.**\n- **⚠️ And what to do instead:** **rather than offering treatments, it may simply be important TO LISTEN.**\n\n- **⚠️ Four slides, one argument:** *the reflex to investigate and prescribe is itself making the patient worse.* **`so-5` and `so-12` are that argument turned into instructions.**\n\nSrc: L3 sl. Obstacles of treatment · 'Sicker than the sick' · \"A reluctance to change\" · \"Caring rather than curing\" · \"The wisdom of doing less\" — all five in full",
