        "h": "Communication disorders — speech against language",
        "body": "### Speech against language — the distinction the deck opens with\n|**Term**|**What it covers**|\n|---|---|\n|**SPEECH**|**the EXPRESSIVE PRODUCTION OF SOUNDS** — an individual's **ARTICULATION · FLUENCY · VOICE · RESONANCE QUALITY**|\n|**LANGUAGE**|**the FORM, FUNCTION and USE of a CONVENTIONAL SYSTEM OF SYMBOLS** — **spoken words, sign language, written words, pictures** — **in a RULE-GOVERNED MANNER for communication**|\n\n- **⚠️ Language is not spoken language.** The definition names **sign language, written words and pictures** explicitly, so **a child with no speech at all may have intact language**, and the two disorders below are not the same disorder.\n\n### The four DSM-5 communication disorders\n|**Disorder**|**What the deck gives**|\n|---|---|\n|**LANGUAGE DISORDER**|⚠️ **named only** — no criterion, no description|\n|**SPEECH SOUND DISORDER**|⚠️ **named only**|\n|**SOCIAL (PRAGMATIC) COMMUNICATION DISORDER**|**PERSISTENT DIFFICULTIES IN THE SOCIAL USE OF VERBAL AND NONVERBAL COMMUNICATION** — and that is the whole slide|\n|**CHILDHOOD-ONSET FLUENCY DISORDER (STUTTERING)**|**DISTURBANCES IN THE NORMAL FLUENCY AND TIME PATTERNING OF SPEECH** that are **INAPPROPRIATE FOR THE INDIVIDUAL'S AGE AND LANGUAGE SKILLS**, and **PERSIST OVER TIME**|\n\n- **⚠️ Two taught, two named:** **two of the four get a slide each and two get only a line in a list.** *Nothing is supplied for language disorder or speech sound disorder — a DSM criterion set written from memory is not a gap-fill.*\n- **⚠️ Hold this against `ch-5`:** **social (pragmatic) communication disorder is the social-communication deficit of autism WITHOUT the restricted, repetitive behaviours.** *That contrast is why DSM-5 created it — and **the deck states neither the contrast nor the reason**, so it is noted here and not built on.*\n\nSrc: L14 sl. The communication disorders · DSM-5: The Communication Disorders · Childhood-Onset Fluency Disorder (Stuttering) · Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder — all four in full",
