        "id": "ch-8",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Tic disorders and Tourette — three decks, three jobs",
        "body": "### What a tic is — the DSM definition\n- **A tic:** **a SUDDEN, RAPID, RECURRENT, NONRHYTHMIC MOTOR MOVEMENT OR VOCALIZATION.**\n- **⚠️ NONRHYTHMIC is the discriminating word** — it is what separates a tic from **tremor**, and it is why the neurology deck's own bedside test (below) is about **suppressibility** rather than rhythm.\n\n### Tourette's disorder — the three criteria\n|**#**|**Criterion**|\n|---|---|\n|**I**|**BOTH MULTIPLE MOTOR *AND* ONE OR MORE VOCAL TICS** have been present at some time during the illness — ⚠️ **although NOT NECESSARILY CONCURRENTLY**|\n|**II**|the tics **MAY WAX AND WANE IN FREQUENCY** but have **PERSISTED FOR MORE THAN 1 YEAR** since first tic onset|\n|**III**|**ONSET IS BEFORE AGE 18 YEARS**|\n\n- **⚠️ The escape clause:** **criterion I is examinable for it** — the motor and vocal tics **need never have occurred at the same time.** A history of motor tics at 7 and a vocal tic at 12 satisfies it.\n\n### ⚠️ What the NEUROLOGY deck adds, and why both are needed\n*`nr-movement` `mv-10` teaches tics from **L3 MOVEMENT DISORDERS**, and the two decks do not overlap.*\n\n|**L14 gives**|**L3 gives (`mv-10`)**|\n|---|---|\n|**the DSM definition** — sudden, rapid, recurrent, nonrhythmic|**the phenomenology** — rapid, coordinated, repetitive, **always the SAME muscle group**: blinking, twisting the neck, shrugging, throat-clearing|\n|**Tourette's three DSM criteria**|**⚠️ THE ONE BEDSIDE FEATURE UNIQUE TO TICS** — they are **the ONLY involuntary movements the patient CAN STOP for a short period.** *That sentence separates a tic from chorea, dystonia, athetosis and tremor*|\n|**onset before 18** (the diagnostic ceiling)|**begins between ages 2 and 13**, and **MORE COMMON IN MALES** (the typical window)|\n\n- **⚠️ Not a conflict:** **2–13 is the usual onset, before 18 is the criterion.** *Both are recorded.*\n- *And the third source: `ps-ocd` `oc-6` separates **tic disorders from OCD** — **tics are INVOLUNTARY and NOT aimed at neutralising anxiety** — while `oc-5` carries the **TIC-RELATED specifier** and the link between **symmetry/ordering compulsions, tics and early onset.* **Three decks, three different jobs, no repetition.***\n- **⚠️ No treatment:** **none for any tic disorder appears in L14.** *`nr-movement` `mv-10` gives treatment for dystonia, not for tics.*\n\nSrc: L14 sl. Tic Disorders, in full including Tourette's three criteria. Comparison columns: L3 via `nr-movement` `mv-10`; L8 via `ps-ocd` `oc-5`/`oc-6` — all read from disk",
