        "h": "BPSD — 90 per cent, and the only symptoms that respond",
        "body": "### The numbers, and the claim that matters most\n- **Frequency:** **BPSD occur in 90 % OF PATIENTS WITH DEMENTIA.**\n- **Who they affect:** they **also CAUSE DISTRESS TO PATIENTS AND CARERS** — not the patient alone.\n- **⚠️⚠️ The line that changes management:** **BPSD, UNLIKE COGNITIVE SYMPTOMS, CAN BE EFFECTIVELY TREATED.** *In a chapter whose cognitive treatments only **slow** the decline (`ge-11`), this is the part of dementia where treatment actually works.*\n\n### They occur in every type — but two types have a signature\n|**Dementia**|**The BPSD that is characteristic**|\n|---|---|\n|**ALZHEIMER'S DISEASE**|BPSD occur, without a named signature|\n|**VASCULAR DEMENTIA**|**EMOTIONAL INSTABILITY is common**|\n|**LEWY BODY DEMENTIA (LBD)**|**VISUAL HALLUCINATION is TYPICALLY SEEN**|\n\n- **⚠️ Clues, not descriptions:** **a demented patient with prominent VISUAL HALLUCINATIONS points to LBD**, and **EMOTIONAL INSTABILITY points to vascular disease.**\n\n### The BPSD list, as the deck groups it\n|**Group**|**Symptoms**|\n|---|---|\n|**The activated group**|**AGGRESSION · SCREAMING · RESTLESSNESS · AGITATION · HOSTILITY · WANDERING · HOARDING**|\n|**The affective and psychotic group**|**APATHY · ANXIETY · MOOD CHANGES · HALLUCINATION · DELUSION · SLEEP DISTURBANCE**|\n\n- *The two-group split is the deck's own line break; it names no label for either. Recorded as the slide sets it out.*\n- *`ps-anxiety` `ax-6` files **DEMENTIA** as the ceiling of the anxiety differential and sends **a newly anxious elderly patient** here and to `nr-delirium`. **`anxiety` is on this list, which is why.***\n- *`ps-ocd` `oc-10` records **HOARDING DISORDER** as a DSM-5 entity named but not described in that chapter. **Hoarding appears here as a BPSD, not as that disorder** — same word, different thing.*\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Behavioural and Psychological Symptoms (BPSD), in full including both symptom groups and the two type-specific signatures",
