        "h": "Three classifications, and the treatable four",
        "body": "### Three classifications, and the deck ranks them\n*\"**Multiple classifications of dementias are present.**\" The deck gives three and names one **the most accurate.***\n\n|**Classification**|**The split**|\n|---|---|\n|**1 — By reversibility**|**TREATABLE (reversible)** against **NON-TREATABLE (irreversible)**|\n|**2 — By age of onset**|**PRESENILE — before 65** against **SENILE — after 65 years old**|\n|**3 — PATHO-ETIOLOGICAL**|⚠️ **the deck's own words: *the most accurate*.** In full in `ge-5`|\n\n### ⚠️ The treatable dementias — four, and this is the list to know\n- **HYPOTHYROIDISM.**\n- **DEFICIENCY IN VITAMIN B12 AND FOLATE.**\n- **NORMAL PRESSURE HYDROCEPHALUS.**\n- **CHRONIC SUBDURAL HAEMATOMA.**\n\n- **⚠️ Why it outweighs its length:** it is **the reason the investigation panel in `ge-10` exists** — **serum B12 and thyroid function are on it precisely because two of these four are found that way**, and **neuroimaging finds the other two.**\n- **⚠️ Treatable and secondary nearly coincide:** **all four reappear in `ge-5`** under **endocrinal, nutritional, traumatic**, and **normal pressure hydrocephalus** as its own entry. **The deck never says so.**\n- *`nr-stroke` `str-10` carries **hydrocephalus** and its drainage among the subarachnoid-haemorrhage complications — **a different hydrocephalus from the normal-pressure one named here**, and the deck defines neither.*\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Classification of dementia, in full including all four treatable causes",
