        "body": "### Risk factors — eight, and the deck flags one and qualifies it\n|**Risk factor**|**Detail**|\n|---|---|\n|**⚠️ AGE**|**mostly affects the elderly population, > 65 years.** ⚠️⚠️ **THE DECK'S OWN N.B.: this is THE MOST IMPORTANT RISK FACTOR — HOWEVER THIS DOES NOT MEAN THAT ANYONE ABOVE 65 WILL SUFFER FROM DEMENTIA**|\n|**DOWN SYNDROME**|named|\n|**DEPRESSION**|named — ⚠️ *and depression is also an early manifestation (`ge-7`) and a differential (`ge-10`). **The deck lists it in all three roles and reconciles them nowhere***|\n|**LOW EDUCATIONAL LEVEL**|named — *the mirror of the protective factors below*|\n|**METABOLIC SYNDROME**|named|\n|**MIDLIFE OBESITY**|named|\n|**SMOKING**|named|\n|**⚠️ ApoE4**|**for LATE-ONSET AD.** ⚠️ **A SINGLE ApoE4 ALLELE DOUBLES THE RISK; TWO ALLELES INCREASE IT 5-FOLD**|\n\n- **⚠️ ApoE4:** **the only quantified genetic risk in the module**, and the dose-response — **×2 for one allele, ×5 for two** — is the examinable form.\n- *`ps-child` `ch-11` records **DOWN SYNDROME** as returning exactly one hit across all 25 decks, **and this row is it.** The deck names it as a dementia risk factor and nowhere else.*\n\n### Potential protective factors — four, numbered\n- **1 — PHYSICAL ACTIVITY.**\n- **2 — HIGH IQ / EDUCATION.**\n- **3 — INTELLECTUAL STIMULATION: READING.**\n- **4 — FOOD RICH IN ANTIOXIDANTS.**\n\n- **⚠️ Two of the four are inverses:** **high IQ/education against low educational level**, and **physical activity against midlife obesity.** *The deck prints both lists and draws no line between them.*\n- **⚠️ Hedged, and unquantified:** **the deck writes *POTENTIAL*** and gives **no effect size, no study, no strength of evidence** for any of the four. *Transcribed as hedged.*\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Risk factors of AD · Potential protective factors for AD — both in full, including the deck's own N.B. on age",
