        "id": "ge-9",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Pathology — three hallmarks and two transmitters",
        "body": "### Gross and histopathology\n- **Gross pathology:** **MARKED BRAIN ATROPHY.**\n- **⚠️ Three hallmarks:** the deck's own phrase is **THE THREE CONSISTENT NEUROPATHOLOGICAL HALLMARKS**, and this is the list to memorise:\n  - **1 — AMYLOID-RICH SENILE PLAQUES.**\n  - **2 — NEUROFIBRILLARY TANGLES.**\n  - **3 — NEURONAL DEGENERATION.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ AND THE TIMING CLAUSE:** these changes **eventually lead to clinical symptoms, but THEY BEGIN YEARS BEFORE THE ONSET OF SYMPTOMS.** *That is the whole justification for `ge-7`'s **mild cognitive impairment** stage, and for a treatment aimed at slowing rather than reversing (`ge-11`).*\n\n### The neurotransmitters — and both drug classes follow from this slide\n|**Transmitter**|**What the deck says**|**Which drug it explains**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**ACETYLCHOLINE (ACh)**|**an IMPORTANT neurotransmitter in areas of the brain involved in MEMORY FORMATION.** ⚠️ **LOSS OF ACh ACTIVITY CORRELATES WITH THE SEVERITY OF AD**|**the CHOLINESTERASE INHIBITORS** (`ge-11`)|\n|**GLUTAMATE**|**also involved in AD** — and no more than that|**MEMANTINE**, an NMDA partial antagonist (`ge-11`)|\n\n- **⚠️ A correlation, not a cause:** **the acetylcholine claim is a CORRELATION WITH SEVERITY** — and it is the strongest mechanistic statement the deck makes. **The glutamate claim is one clause with no mechanism at all**, yet it carries a whole drug.\n- *`nr-lmn` `lm-9` uses **acetylcholinesterase inhibitors** for an entirely different purpose — **pyridostigmine** in myasthenia gravis, and **edrophonium** as a diagnostic test — with doses printed. **Same enzyme, same class, different organ. Neither chapter repeats the other.***\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Pathology of AD, in full including the gross, histopathological and neurotransmitter blocks",
