        "id": "ge-11",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Treatment — what slows it, and what nothing touches",
        "body": "### The first line, and it is not a drug\n- **First line:** **TREATMENT OF THE UNDERLYING CAUSE OF SECONDARY DEMENTIA.** *That is `ge-4`'s treatable four and `ge-5`'s eleven secondary causes — **the reason the whole classification exists.***\n\n### A) Non-pharmacological treatment of primary dementia — four\n- **COGNITIVE ENHANCEMENT.**\n- **INDIVIDUAL AND GROUP THERAPY.**\n- **ENVIRONMENTAL MODIFICATION.**\n- **ATTENTION TO SAFETY.**\n\n- *✅ `ps-therapy` `th-4` and `th-6` already file this row — **individual and group therapy under the non-pharmacological treatment of primary dementia**, cited to this deck. **It is paid from both ends and not repeated here.***\n\n### B) Pharmacological treatment — two classes, and each has a ceiling\n|**Class**|**Agents**|**Mechanism, as printed**|**Where it works**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**CHOLINESTERASE INHIBITORS**|**DONEPEZIL · RIVASTIGMINE · GALANTAMINE**|**inhibit acetylcholinesterase activity** — they **block the esterase-mediated metabolism of ACETYLCHOLINE to CHOLINE and ACETATE**, which **results in INCREASED ACETYLCHOLINE IN THE SYNAPTIC CLEFT**|⚠️ **the ONLY PROVED treatment for MILD TO MODERATE dementia**|\n|**MEMANTINE**|—|**an NMDA (N-methyl-D-aspartate, one of the GLUTAMATE receptors) PARTIAL ANTAGONIST**|⚠️ **proved effective in MODERATE TO SEVERE cases**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ The sentence that defines it:** **acetylcholinesterase inhibitors SLOW PROGRESSION BUT DO NOT REVERSE the degenerative process.** *Which follows directly from `ge-9`: the pathology begins years before symptoms.*\n- **⚠️ Tacrine:** **WITHDRAWN DUE TO HEPATOTOXICITY.** *The deck's only withdrawn drug, and its only adverse-effect statement in this chapter.*\n- **⚠️ Staged, not combined:** **cholinesterase inhibitors mild-to-moderate, memantine moderate-to-severe.** *That is the whole prescribing rule the deck gives.*\n- *⚠️ **MEMANTINE appears twice in this module with two different indications:** here for moderate-to-severe AD, and in `ps-ocd` `oc-8` as **an AUGMENTING AGENT in the SSRI-refractory patient**, where `oc-4`'s **glutamate-up** model explains it. **Same drug, same receptor, different disease.***\n\n### For the BPSD — and the deck hedges hard\n- **ANTIDEPRESSANTS and ANTIPSYCHOTICS:** to be used **CAUTIOUSLY** and **WITH THE LOWEST POSSIBLE DOSES WHEN NEEDED**, **TO PREVENT SEDATION** — the deck names **RISPERIDONE and HALOPERIDOL.**\n- *Antipsychotic classes and their adverse effects are `ps-pharm`; **`ps-schizmgmt` carries risperidone and haloperidol in their own indication.** Not repeated here.*\n\n### C) Care for caregivers\n- **⚠️ The figure:** **ONE THIRD OF CAREGIVERS DEVELOP DEPRESSION.** *The deck makes caregiver care a lettered arm of treatment on its own, alongside the drugs, and gives that one figure as the whole justification.*\n\n### ⚠️ And the deck closes on a negative\n- **The closing negative:** **\"TILL NOW, THERE IS NO PROVED TREATMENT TO SLOW PROGRESSION OF MILD COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENT.\"** *The stage `ge-7` names as the one that can precede mild AD is the one stage with nothing to offer.*\n- **⚠️ No doses:** **none is printed for ANY of the six named drugs.** **None is supplied.**\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Treatment of AD · B) Pharmacological treatment · the memantine and antipsychotic slide · C) Care for caregivers, and the closing N.B. — all in full",
