        "h": "What delirium is, its six names, and two sources that agree",
        "body": "### What it is\n- **A NEUROPSYCHIATRIC SYNDROME**, **common among the MEDICALLY ILL.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ The sentence it turns on:** it is **OFTEN MISDIAGNOSED AS A PSYCHIATRIC ILLNESS, WHICH CAN RESULT IN DELAY OF ADEQUATE MEDICAL INTERVENTION.** **SIGNIFICANT MORTALITY IS ASSOCIATED WITH DELIRIUM, SO IDENTIFYING IT IS CRUCIAL.**\n- *That is the `ps-osce` `os-1` instruction with a body count attached: **to be a good psychiatrist you have to be a good physician.***\n\n### The definition\n- **The definition:** **A TRANSIENT, USUALLY REVERSIBLE, CEREBRAL DYSFUNCTION** that **manifests clinically with A BROAD RANGE OF NEUROPSYCHIATRIC ABNORMALITIES.**\n- **⚠️ THE HALLMARKS: WAXING AND WANING SIGNS, SYMPTOMS AND SENSORIUM.** **ALERTNESS AND VIGILANCE IN THE PATIENT FLUCTUATE.**\n- **⚠️ And the deck's own summary line:** **DELIRIUM IS LIFE-THREATENING BUT CAN BE PREVENTED.**\n\n### Six names for one thing\n|**Name**|**What it emphasises**|\n|---|---|\n|**ACUTE CONFUSIONAL STATE**|the cognitive disturbance|\n|**ICU PSYCHOSIS**|the setting where it is commonest|\n|**SUNDOWNING**|the diurnal pattern — *and the deck uses this word again as a clinical feature, `dl-4`*|\n|**ENCEPHALOPATHY**|the diffuse brain dysfunction|\n|**ACUTE BRAIN FAILURE**|the organ-failure framing|\n|**SYNDROME OF CEREBRAL INSUFFICIENCY**|the same, in older language|\n\n- **⚠️ `ICU PSYCHOSIS`:** **a name for DELIRIUM, not a psychosis.** *The nomenclature is itself the misdiagnosis the deck warns about.*\n\n### Where the book puts it — a second source, and it agrees\n- **The book's own panel:** **an ACUTE DISTURBANCE OF ATTENTION associated with DISTURBANCE IN COGNITION, with CLOUDING OF CONSCIOUSNESS, PERCEPTION AND ORIENTATION → REDUCED ABILITY TO SUSTAIN ATTENTION TO ENVIRONMENTAL STIMULI.**\n- **⚠️ And it places it anatomically:** consciousness needs **AROUSAL** and **CONTENT**, and **AROUSAL DAMAGE → COMA, SEMI-COMA, DELIRIUM**, while **CONTENT DAMAGE → DEMENTIA, DELUSIONS, CONFUSION, INATTENTION.** *The deck never does this. Full spectrum and neuroanatomy in `nr-coma`.*\n- **⚠️ A stage, not only a state:** in **TOXIC-METABOLIC COMA** the book says *a confusional state or delirium occurs initially*, and **CONFUSION AND STUPOR COMMONLY PRECEDE MOTOR SIGNS.** **The same patient, further along, is `nr-coma`'s.**\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Delirium ×3 (the syndrome, Nomenclature and Definition), all in full · book Neurology ch.11 printed p.99 (the boxed panel and the arousal/content split) and p.100 (metabolic coma's differentiating features)",
