        "h": "Epidemiology — 10 per cent on the ward, 87 in intensive care",
        "body": "### The four figures the deck prints\n|**Population**|**Figure**|\n|---|---|\n|**Hospitalized medical patients — present on admission**|**10 %**|\n|**Hospitalized medical patients — develop it AFTER admission**|**a further 10–30 %**|\n|**⚠️ POSTOPERATIVE**|**15–53 % of patients**|\n|**⚠️⚠️ INTENSIVE CARE**|**70–87 %**|\n\n- **⚠️ The gradient is the point:** **10 % on a medical ward, up to 87 % in intensive care.** *That is why `ICU psychosis` became one of its names (`dl-1`).*\n- **⚠️ Read the second row carefully:** the **10–30 %** is **incident**, developing *after* admission — **so it is acquired in hospital**, which is what makes the preventable claim of `dl-1` meaningful.\n- *⚠️ **`ps-geri` `ge-1` records the same figure from the other side of this deck:** delirium is **present in 10 % of hospitalized medical patients** is the one epidemiological statement `L13` makes about either half.*\n\n### ⚠️ And one slide of the epidemiology block is a single word\n- **⚠️ One slide, one word:** **the slide after the epidemiology figures carries `FEVER` and nothing else.** **Measured: one word of text.** *Whether it heads a cause list, a case or an illustration is not recoverable from the cache.* **Recorded, not guessed** — and **fever does appear in `dl-3`'s aetiology as `Infections`**, the likeliest home for it.\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Epidemiology of delirium, in full · the one-word slide following it, measured on the PDF page by page",
