        "h": "Clinical characteristics, and the subtype that gets missed",
        "body": "### The seven characteristics\n|**#**|**Feature**|\n|---|---|\n|**1**|**DEVELOPS ACUTELY — HOURS TO DAYS**|\n|**2**|**⚠️ FLUCTUATING LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS DURING THE DAY.** **Cognitive functions are usually WORSE IN THE EVENING — the SUNDOWNING PHENOMENON**|\n|**3**|**⚠️ REDUCED ABILITY TO FOCUS, MAINTAIN OR SHIFT ATTENTION**|\n|**4**|**AGITATION** or **HYPERSOMNOLENCE** — the three subtypes below|\n|**5**|**EXTREME EMOTIONAL LABILITY**|\n|**6**|**A CHANGE IN COGNITION or DEVELOPMENT OF PERCEPTUAL DISTURBANCES** that is **NOT BETTER ACCOUNTED FOR by a pre-existing, established or evolving DEMENTIA**|\n|**7**|**⚠️ EVIDENCE from the HISTORY, PHYSICAL EXAMINATION or LABS that the disturbance is caused by the PHYSIOLOGICAL CONSEQUENCE OF A MEDICAL CONDITION**|\n\n### ⚠️ The three motor subtypes\n|**Subtype**|**How it presents**|\n|---|---|\n|**HYPERACTIVE**|**AGITATION**|\n|**HYPOACTIVE**|**HYPERSOMNOLENCE**|\n|**MIXED**|**both hyperactive and hypoactive**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ The dangerous one:** **HYPOACTIVE**, because it is the subtype that looks like nothing — *and it is exactly the case `dl-1` warns is **misdiagnosed as a psychiatric illness**, delaying medical intervention.* **The deck names the three subtypes and comments on none of them.**\n- **⚠️ Six and seven are not description:** they are **EXCLUSION and ATTRIBUTION** — **6 excludes dementia** (worked in `dl-8`) and **7 requires a medical cause to be DEMONSTRATED**, not assumed.\n- **⚠️ Attention is characteristic 3:** and it is **what the testing in `dl-7` measures** — *the book's own definition calls delirium **an acute disturbance of ATTENTION** before it mentions anything else (`dl-1`).*\n- *`ps-symptom` `sy-8` files **ATTENTION** in the intellectual-functions grid and **`sy-9`** treats **disorientation and recent amnesia** as markers of **ORGANICITY**. **That is this chapter, seen from the mental-state examination.***\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Clinical characteristics, in full — all seven, and all three subtypes",
