        "h": "Course and prognosis — half, a quarter, a quarter",
        "body": "### The course\n- **The course:** **symptoms usually CONTINUE TO PROGRESS OR FLUCTUATE UNTIL TREATMENT OF THE UNDERLYING CAUSE.** *Which is why `dl-9`'s first line is not a drug.*\n- **⚠️ Timing of resolution:** symptoms usually **RESOLVE WITHIN A WEEK of correction or improvement of the underlying aetiology** — **HOWEVER symptoms MAY WAX AND WANE**, and **in some patients it can take WEEKS to resolve.**\n\n### The four outcome figures\n|**Outcome**|**Figure**|\n|---|---|\n|**IMPROVE**|**about 50 % of patients**|\n|**⚠️ NEVER RETURN TO BASELINE**|**about 25 % of cases** — **particularly OLDER patients**|\n|**⚠️ DEATH**|**about 25 % of patients**|\n|**Time to resolution**|**usually within a week; sometimes weeks**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ They sum to 100 %:** **HALF IMPROVE, A QUARTER ARE PERMANENTLY CHANGED, A QUARTER DIE.** *Set that against `dl-1`'s claim that delirium is **usually reversible** — **both are the deck's own, printed four slides apart**, and it reconciles them nowhere. **Recorded, not corrected.***\n- *⚠️ **`ps-geri` `ge-6` records the same arithmetic problem in the other half of this deck** — its dementia prevalence figures sum to between 110 % and 165 %. **Here the figures close exactly; there they do not.***\n- **⚠️ The bridge to `dl-8`:** **the 25 % who never return to baseline** — *a patient who does not recover has, by the end, something that looks like dementia.* **And the deck's own N.B. is that the two coexist.**\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Course and prognosis, in full — every figure",
