        "h": "Delirium against dementia — and the N.B. that undoes the table",
        "body": "### The three differentials the deck names\n- **The three:** **DEMENTIA · DEPRESSION · OTHER PSYCHIATRIC DISORDERS.**\n- **⚠️ Only the first is worked.** *Depression and \"other psychiatric disorders\" are named and given no discriminator — and **`ps-geri` `ge-10` runs the same differential from the dementia side**, where **DELIRIUM is its number 3** and **depression in the elderly is number 4, as PSEUDO-DEMENTIA.*** **The two halves of this deck point at each other and neither closes the loop.**\n\n### ⚠️ The table — the single highest-yield object in the chapter\n|**Feature**|**DELIRIUM**|**DEMENTIA**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**ONSET**|**ACUTE**|**INSIDIOUS**|\n|**COURSE**|**FLUCTUATING**|**PROGRESSIVE**|\n|**DURATION**|**DAYS–WEEKS**|**MONTHS–YEARS**|\n|**⚠️ CONSCIOUSNESS**|**ALTERED**|**CLEAR**|\n|**ATTENTION**|**IMPAIRED**|**NORMAL — unless severe**|\n|**PSYCHOMOTOR CHANGES**|**INCREASED OR DECREASED**|**OFTEN NORMAL**|\n|**REVERSIBILITY**|**USUALLY**|**RARELY**|\n\n### What the two share\n- **Shared by both:** **MEMORY · HALLUCINATIONS (VISUAL) · DELUSION · SUNDOWNER.**\n- **⚠️ Sundowning does not discriminate:** it is **on the *common* list**, and it is **also one of delirium's six NAMES** (`dl-1`) **and one of its seven clinical characteristics** (`dl-5`).\n- **⚠️ VISUAL hallucination is specified**, and it is shared. *`ps-geri` `ge-3` records that **visual hallucination is the BPSD signature of LEWY BODY dementia** — so in a demented patient it points to a subtype, and in an acutely confused one it points nowhere.*\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ CONSCIOUSNESS is the discriminator, and the module uses it three times\n- **ALTERED in delirium, CLEAR in dementia** — and:\n  - **`ps-geri` `ge-7`** makes **CLEAR CONSCIOUSNESS** *gate 2* of the Alzheimer's diagnosis.\n  - **`ps-osce` `os-2`** makes **CONSCIOUSNESS** item 3 of ten in the General Look, **before anything psychiatric is assessed at all.**\n  - **`nr-coma`** is what happens when it fails altogether.\n\n### ⚠️ And the deck's closing N.B., which undoes the neatness\n- **DELIRIUM AND DEMENTIA CAN COEXIST — IN FACT, DELIRIUM IS VERY COMMON IN DEMENTED PATIENTS.**\n- *So the table sorts two pure presentations, and the commonest real patient is both.* **That is the deck's own warning, printed immediately under its own table.**\n\nSrc: L13 sl. Differential diagnosis, in full — the three differentials, the seven-row table, the common-features list and the closing N.B.",
