        "w": "must",
        "h": "The spectrum, and three things coma is not",
        "body": "### The five states, in order\n|**State**|**Definition as printed**|\n|---|---|\n|**ALERT, CONSCIOUS**|**a perfectly normal state of arousal**|\n|**LETHARGY**|**lies between alertness and stupor** — **difficulty MAINTAINING alertness**|\n|**STUPOR**|**a state of BASELINE UNRESPONSIVENESS requiring REPEATED APPLICATION OF VIGOROUS STIMULI — verbal or motor — to achieve arousal**|\n|**SEMI-COMA**|**a state of response to MOTOR STIMULUS ONLY, not verbal**|\n|**COMA**|**a state of COMPLETE UNRESPONSIVENESS to arousal**|\n\n- **⚠️ Defined by the stimulus:** **the ladder is WHAT IT TAKES TO GET A RESPONSE**, not what the patient looks like — **verbal at stupor, motor only at semi-coma, nothing at coma.**\n- **⚠️ And the book warns against its own words:** `nr-comapproach` `ca-3` records its instruction that **it is MORE USEFUL TO DESCRIBE THE PATIENT'S SPONTANEOUS BEHAVIOUR AND RESPONSES TO STIMULI than to use terms such as *stupor* or *obtunded*.** *It prints the vocabulary and then tells you to prefer description. **Both are transcribed.***\n\n### ⚠️ Three things the book insists coma is NOT\n|**State**|**What the book says**|\n|---|---|\n|**SLEEP**|**THE ONLY NORMAL FORM OF ALTERED CONSCIOUSNESS**|\n|**DELIRIUM**|**an ACUTE DISTURBANCE OF ATTENTION** with **disturbance in cognition** and **CLOUDING OF CONSCIOUSNESS, PERCEPTION AND ORIENTATION** → **reduced ability to sustain attention to environmental stimuli**|\n|**ENCEPHALOPATHY**|**a term for ANY DIFFUSE DISEASE OF THE BRAIN that ALTERS BRAIN FUNCTION OR STRUCTURE**|\n\n- *⚠️ **`nr-delirium` is the whole of row 2** — six names, the fluctuating sensorium, the three motor subtypes and the dementia table. **Not repeated here.** ⚠️ And note that **`encephalopathy` is one of delirium's own six names in `L13`**, while the book makes it a far wider term. **Two sources, two scopes, both recorded.***\n\nSrc: book Neurology ch.11 printed p.99, in full — the five-state spectrum and the boxed \"You should differentiate between…!\" panel",
