        "h": "The ARAS, and the only two ways to be comatose",
        "body": "### The ascending reticular activating system\n- **Where it is:** the **CORE OF THE BRAINSTEM.**\n- **What it receives:** **input from SOMATIC AFFERENTS** — ⚠️ **it ACTS AS A GATE.**\n- **Where it projects:** to the **THALAMUS and the LIMBIC SYSTEM** → **BOTH CEREBRAL CORTICES.**\n- *The figure adds the afferent streams by name: **visual impulses from the eyes**, **auditory and vestibular impulses**, **somatic sensory impulses from nociceptors, proprioceptors and touch receptors** — and the **reticulospinal tracts** running back down to skeletal muscle.*\n- *⚠️ `nr-intro` teaches the thalamus as the relay for **all sensory input except olfactory**; this is the same structure seen from the arousal side. Not repeated here.*\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ Coma pathogenesis — and there are only two ways to be comatose\n- **COMA IMPLIES DYSFUNCTION OF: THE ARAS, *OR* BOTH HEMI-CORTICES.**\n- **Anatomically:**\n  - **CENTRAL BRAINSTEM STRUCTURES, BILATERALLY** — **from the CAUDAL MEDULLA to the ROSTRAL MIDBRAIN.**\n  - **BOTH HEMISPHERES.**\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ The word *BOTH* does all the work**, and it is the chapter's single most useful rule: **a unilateral hemispheric lesion — however large — does not by itself cause coma.** *So a hemiplegic patient who is awake is `nr-hemi`; the same patient unconscious has either **brainstem involvement** or **a second hemisphere problem, most often raised pressure and herniation.***\n- **⚠️ It shapes the classification:** **toxic-metabolic causes hit both hemispheres diffusely; structural causes must either be bilateral, raise the pressure, or sit in the brainstem** (`co-4` to `co-6`).\n\nSrc: book Neurology ch.11 printed p.100, in full — the ARAS description, every label of the sagittal figure, and the pathogenesis block",
