        "id": "co-6",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Structural coma — one third, and the pupil that ends the argument",
        "body": "### The share, and the two ways a structural lesion causes coma\n- **⚠️ STRUCTURAL COMA IS ONE THIRD OF CASES.**\n- **SUPRATENTORIAL lesions cause coma by** — **WIDESPREAD BILATERAL DISEASE**, **INCREASED INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE**, or **HERNIATION.**\n- **INFRATENTORIAL lesions** — **involve the RAS, usually with ASSOCIATED BRAINSTEM SIGNS.**\n- **General features:** **ASYMMETRIC MOTOR OR OCULAR REFLEX FINDINGS OCCUR EARLY.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ Printed twice, in red the second time:** **A UNILATERAL DILATED PUPIL UNRESPONSIVE TO LIGHT INDICATES UNCAL HERNIATION UNTIL PROVEN OTHERWISE.**\n\n### The three structural pictures, and how they differ\n|**Type**|**Differential characteristics**|**Causes named**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**SUPRATENTORIAL MASS**|**initiating signs usually of FOCAL CEREBRAL DYSFUNCTION** — they **point to ONE ANATOMIC AREA.** **Motor signs OFTEN ASYMMETRICAL**|**HAEMATOMA · NEOPLASM · ABSCESS · CONTUSION · VASCULAR ACCIDENTS · DIFFUSE AXONAL DAMAGE**|\n|**INFRATENTORIAL MASS**|**history of PRECEDING BRAINSTEM DYSFUNCTION or SUDDEN ONSET of coma.** ⚠️ **Localizing brainstem signs precede or accompany onset and ALWAYS INCLUDE OCULOVESTIBULAR ABNORMALITY.** **CRANIAL NERVE PALSIES usually present.** ⚠️ **\"BIZARRE\" RESPIRATORY PATTERNS are common, usually present AT ONSET**|**NEOPLASM · VASCULAR ACCIDENTS · TRAUMA · CEREBELLAR HAEMORRHAGE · CENTRAL PONTINE MYELINOLYSIS**|\n|**INFECTION-INDUCED**|**HISTORY OF INFECTION · FEVER · NUCHAL RIGIDITY (meningeal signs) · KERNIG and BRUDZINSKI SIGNS · SKIN RASH**|—|\n\n- **⚠️ The infection row is `nr-cns` entire**, seen from the coma side. *`ci-2` defines both meningeal signs and works the case; `ci-5` records the **purpuric rash of MENINGOCOCCAL meningitis**, which is this row's *skin rash*. **Not repeated here.***\n- **⚠️ The infratentorial rule to carry:** ***always include oculovestibular abnormality*** — **an eye-movement abnormality in a comatose patient localises to the brainstem** (`nr-comapproach` `ca-4`).\n\n### C — Psychiatric causes\n- **CATATONIA** — **MUTE, WITH MARKED DECREASE IN MOTOR ACTIVITY.**\n- *⚠️ `ps-emerg` `em-7` teaches catatonia in full, including its workup and treatment, and records that **non-convulsive status epilepticus can present as an unresponsive, immobile patient** — which is `co-8`'s row and the reason `em-7` demands an EEG.*\n\nSrc: book Neurology ch.11 printed p.101 (the supra/infratentorial split and the tentorium figure) and p.102 (the three differential-characteristic blocks and the psychiatric cause), both in full",
