        "id": "co-8",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "PVS, locked-in, and the two mimics that need an EEG",
        "body": "### Persistent vegetative state — *\"defect in the content, not in the awareness\"*\n- **Appears INTERMITTENTLY AWAKE.**\n- **BREATHES SPONTANEOUSLY**, with **intact cardiac and gastric motility.**\n- **Movement:** **exhibits PRIMITIVE REFLEXES or EYE-ROVING BEHAVIOUR.**\n- **SEVERE CEREBRAL DAMAGE PERSISTS.**\n- **⚠️ The exclusion:** **NO MEANINGFUL COMMUNICATION OR CORTICAL RESPONSIVENESS OCCURS.**\n- **⚠️ A CHRONIC, NOT ACUTE, EVENT** — with **impaired CONTENT of consciousness (cortex)**. *That is `co-1`'s table exactly: **content lost, arousal preserved.***\n\n### The eight diagnostic criteria\n- **NO AWARENESS of self or environment** · **NO INTERACTION with others** · **NO evidence of SUSTAINED, REPRODUCIBLE, PURPOSEFUL or VOLUNTARY behavioural responses** to visual, auditory, tactile or noxious stimuli · **NO evidence of LANGUAGE COMPREHENSION OR EXPRESSION** · **INTERMITTENT WAKEFULNESS** manifested by **SLEEP/WAKE CYCLES** · **BOWEL AND BLADDER INCONTINENCE** · **PRESERVED AUTONOMIC FUNCTIONS** · **VARIABLY PRESERVED CRANIAL NERVE reflexes** — pupillary, oculocephalic, corneal, vestibulo-ocular and gag — **and SPINAL reflexes.**\n\n### Locked-in syndrome\n- **⚠️ The trap:** **THE PATIENT MAY APPEAR IN A PERSISTENT VEGETATIVE STATE**, and is not.\n- **De-efferented:** **unable to move the limbs and face, or gaze laterally.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ What survives:** **VERTICAL GAZE AND EYE BLINKING**, and **communication and cortical function are preserved ONLY by blink and vertical gaze.**\n- **The code:** **the patient BLINKS ONCE FOR *YES* AND TWICE FOR *NO*.**\n- **⚠️ Cause: EXTENSIVE PONTINE INFARCTION.** *The MRI figure labels the **preserved reticular activating system behind the pons** — which is why arousal survives.*\n\n### Non-convulsive status epilepticus, and psychiatric unresponsiveness\n|**Mimic**|**What the book gives**|\n|---|---|\n|**NON-CONVULSIVE STATUS EPILEPTICUS**|**continued or persistent GENERALIZED SEIZURES in the ABSENCE of obvious clinical convulsive activity.** Subtle signs: **RHYTHMICAL NYSTAGMUS · TWITCHING of an eyelid or part of the face or limb.** ⚠️ **Obtain an EMERGENT EEG and assess the response to an IV BENZODIAZEPINE BOLUS**|\n|**PSYCHIATRIC UNRESPONSIVENESS**|**RARE**, and **not diagnosed except after exclusion of all organic causes.** ⚠️ **A DIAGNOSIS OF EXCLUSION** — **no history of drug overdose · INTACT BRAINSTEM REFLEXES · SPONTANEOUS BREATHING · no focal neurological deficit · NORMAL METABOLIC PROFILE · NORMAL BRAIN MRI with DW sequence · NORMAL EEG, SIMILAR TO THE AWAKE STATE**|\n\n- *⚠️ **`nr-epilepsy` `ep-10` teaches status epilepticus and its protocol; `ps-emerg` `em-7` records that non-convulsive status can present as an unresponsive, immobile patient and is why catatonia needs an EEG. Three chapters, one entity, no repetition.***\n\n### ⚠️ The three-way table the book prints\n|**Feature**|**BRAIN DEATH**|**PERSISTENT VEGETATIVE STATE**|**LOCKED-IN SYNDROME**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**BRAINSTEM REFLEXES**|**ABSENT**|**VARIABLY PRESERVED**|**PRESERVED**|\n|**AWARENESS / CONTENT**|**NONE**|**ABSENT**|**⚠️ FULLY PRESERVED**|\n|**MOTOR FUNCTION**|**NONE**|**PRIMITIVE REFLEXES**|**VERTICAL GAZE / BLINK ONLY**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ Read the middle row across:** **the locked-in patient is FULLY AWARE**, and is the one state on this page where **the person is present and the examination is nearly empty.** *That is why the book calls the eye gaze **the key**.*\n\nSrc: book Neurology ch.11 printed p.104 (PVS and its eight criteria), p.105 (locked-in, NCSE and psychiatric unresponsiveness) and p.106 (the three-way comparison table and the \"identifying the mimics\" panel) — all in full",
