        "w": "must",
        "h": "The golden rules, and the initial approach",
        "body": "### The golden rules, as the book prints them\n- **⚠️ Rule one:** **ALL ALTERATIONS IN AROUSAL CONSTITUTE ACUTE LIFE-THREATENING EMERGENCIES.** There is **always an urgency**: determine **the underlying disease process** and **the direction in which it is evolving**, and **protect the brain against more serious or irreversible damage.**\n- **NO MANAGEMENT OF COMA AT HOME.**\n- **⚠️ ABC's ARE FIRST PRIORITY** — **AIRWAY, BREATHING, CIRCULATION** — **identify and address life-threatening inadequacies.**\n- **Oxygen:** **MUST ENSURE IT REACHES CNS AND VITAL ORGANS.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ Order of business:** **ADDRESS IMMEDIATELY LIFE-THREATENING CONDITIONS — CARDIAC, METABOLIC — BEFORE ADDRESSING THE CNS.** *The book's own gloss: **try to go in a more generalized rather than a neurological approach.***\n- **Treat fast movers:** **RAPIDLY PROGRESSIVE METABOLIC DISORDERS — HYPOGLYCAEMIA.**\n- **Then the pressure:** **EVALUATE FOR INTRACRANIAL HYPERTENSION AND IMMINENT HERNIATION**, and treat (`ca-5`).\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ The rule that surprises:** *in a neurology chapter, the instruction is **not to start with the neurology.*** **It is `ps-osce` `os-1`'s BODY–MIND rule with the priority made explicit.**\n\n### The initial approach — five steps\n|**#**|**Step**|**Detail**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**1**|**ABC**|**maintain AIRWAY, BREATHING and CIRCULATION**|\n|**2**|**URGENTLY CORRECT ANY HYPOTHERMIA**|*and `nr-coma` `co-7` explains why it cannot wait: **hypothermia mimics brain death***|\n|**3**|**ESTABLISH STABILITY OF THE CERVICAL SPINE**|**if trauma has occurred or is strongly suspected**|\n|**4**|**⚠️ RULE OUT HYPOGLYCAEMIA**|**an IMMEDIATE FINGERSTICK GLUCOSE**, or **EMPIRICAL INFUSION OF 50 % DEXTROSE WITH IV THIAMINE — AT LEAST 100 mg**|\n|**5**|**BASIC BLOODS AND URINE DRUG SCREEN**|**CBC · ELECTROLYTES · GLUCOSE · RENAL AND LIVER FUNCTIONS · PT, PTT, INR · ABG** · **possible CARBON MONOXIDE level if CO poisoning suspected**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ The only quantified thiamine:** ***at least 100 mg***, and **it is given WITH the dextrose, not after it.** *`ps-sud` `sd-8`, `ps-geri` `ge-5` and `nr-delirium` `dl-3` all name Wernicke–Korsakoff and none gives a dose. **This is the one place in the module that does.***\n\n### The ABC panel, in the book's own three columns\n|**Airway**|**Breathing**|**Circulation**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**TALKING = GOOD**|**LOOK: work of breathing, respiratory rate**|**LOOK: mental status, colour**|\n|**LOOK: oedema, blood, vomitus, foreign body**|**LISTEN: breath sounds**|**FEEL: peripheral pulses**|\n|**LISTEN: NOISY = OBSTRUCTED**|**CHECK: pulse oximetry**|**CHECK: heart rate, cardiac rhythm, blood pressure**|\n\n### Airway and circulation, managed\n- **AIRWAY —** **is it patent?** · **is there trauma or foreign body obstructing it?** · **try CHIN LIFT to help open it — ⚠️ PROTECT THE CERVICAL SPINE** · **place a NASAL or ORAL airway if indicated** · **INTUBATION.**\n- **CIRCULATION — is the patient in shock?** Check **pulses, heart rate, blood pressure, perfusion.** ⚠️ **REMEMBER HYPOTENSION IS A LATE SIGN OF SHOCK.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ The fluid rule, against instinct:** **DO NOT RESTRICT FLUIDS in comatose patients with inadequate intravascular volume** — **CARDIAC OUTPUT AND CEREBRAL PERFUSION ARE MUCH MORE IMPORTANT THAN FLUID RESTRICTION.** **Use ISOTONIC solutions and blood as indicated; DO NOT USE HYPOTONIC SOLUTIONS to treat shock, particularly in coma or possible cerebral oedema.**\n- **Haemorrhage:** **IDENTIFY IT ANYWHERE AND CONTROL IT** — **especially if trauma is the cause.**\n\nSrc: book Neurology ch.11 printed p.107 (golden rules, initial approach and the ABC pictogram panel) and p.108 (airway and circulation management), both in full",
