        "w": "must",
        "h": "History — sudden, acute, and the four drugs to ask about",
        "body": "### A — Sudden onset\n|**Cause**|**Detail**|\n|---|---|\n|**INTRACRANIAL HAEMORRHAGE**|⚠️ **a PRODROMAL SEVERE HEADACHE may accompany SUBARACHNOID HAEMORRHAGE from a ruptured intracranial aneurysm**, or occur with cerebral haemorrhage|\n|**CARDIOPULMONARY ARREST**|**significant CEREBRAL HYPOPERFUSION**|\n|**EMBOLIC**|**MULTIPLE CEREBRAL INFARCTS**|\n|**TRAUMA**|**OBSERVED OR UNWITNESSED HEAD TRAUMA**|\n\n- *⚠️ **`nr-stroke` `str-10` is the first row in full** — the thunderclap headache of SAH and its three complications with clocks, including hydrocephalus and external ventricular drainage. **Not repeated here.***\n\n### B — Acute onset, *confusion or delirium preceding coma*\n- **TOXO-METABOLIC AETIOLOGY** — **ORGAN DYSFUNCTION or INFECTION · ELECTROLYTE DISORDERS · MEDICINAL OR DRUG TOXICITY.**\n- **CRITICAL BRAINSTEM INFARCTION.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ THE PARENTHESIS IS THE DIAGNOSIS.** ***Confusion or delirium preceding coma*** **is `nr-coma` `co-4`'s first differentiating feature written into the history**, and it is the single most useful question to ask a relative: **was there a period of confusion first?** *If yes, the coma is probably metabolic; if it was sudden, section A.*\n\n### C — What to obtain immediately\n- **CURRENT MEDICATIONS** — ⚠️ **especially any DIABETIC, ANTICONVULSANT, CARDIAC drugs, or WARFARIN.** Any history of **adverse or allergic medicinal reactions.**\n- Any history of **RECENT HEAD TRAUMA, FEBRILE OR OTHER ILLNESS, or PREVIOUS NEUROLOGICAL SYMPTOMS.**\n- **Any use of RECREATIONAL DRUGS.**\n\n- **⚠️ Each drug class is a mechanism:** **diabetic drugs → hypoglycaemia** (`ca-1`) · **anticonvulsants → toxicity, and non-adherence → status** (`nr-epilepsy` `ep-10`) · **cardiac drugs → arrhythmia and digitalis toxicity** · **warfarin → intracranial haemorrhage.** *The book names the drugs and leaves the reasoning; it is set out here because each changes the first investigation.*\n- *⚠️ `nr-delirium` `dl-4` lists **twelve drug classes** that cause an acute confusional state, **and the book's four are the ones that cause it fastest.***\n\nSrc: book Neurology ch.11 printed p.108 (History A and B) and p.109 (History C), both in full",
