        "id": "ca-3",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Examination — vitals, general look, and the Glasgow Coma Scale",
        "body": "### 1 — Vital signs, and what each abnormality means\n|**Sign**|**Consider**|\n|---|---|\n|**HYPERTENSION TO AN EXTREME DEGREE**|**ACUTE CEREBRAL HAEMORRHAGE OR INFARCTION · HYPERTENSIVE ENCEPHALOPATHY · COCAINE ABUSE · ECLAMPSIA**|\n|**HYPOTENSION**|**HYPOVOLAEMIA · CARDIOGENIC SHOCK · SEPTIC SHOCK**|\n|**FEVER**|**INFECTION · HEAT STROKE**|\n|**HYPOTHERMIA**|**COLD EXPOSURE — especially with ALCOHOLISM · HYPOTHYROIDISM · HYPOGLYCAEMIA · SEPSIS · infrequently, a PRIMARY BRAIN LESION**|\n|**⚠️ PULSE ASYMMETRY**|**DISSECTING ANEURYSM**|\n\n- **⚠️ Hypothermia, three times:** here, on **`nr-coma` `co-7`'s brain-death exclusions**, and as **`ca-1`'s step 2.** *One instruction: **correct it before you conclude anything.***\n\n### 2 — General look\n- **HEAD:** **inspect and palpate the scalp for signs of trauma.**\n  - **⚠️ BLOOD IN THE EXTERNAL EAR CANAL — BATTLE SIGN** — or **AROUND THE EYES — RACCOON SIGN** — **SUGGESTS SKULL FRACTURE.**\n  - **Inspect ears and nose for BLOOD AND CSF.**\n- **CUTANEOUS BLEEDING:**\n  - **Around the eyes or mastoid area:** **→ SKULL FRACTURES**\n  - **DIFFUSE HAEMATOMAS → SYSTEMIC BLEEDING**\n  - **⚠️ Splinter or palmar haemorrhages:** **→ INFECTIVE ENDOCARDITIS**\n- **JAUNDICE AND ASCITES → HEPATIC COMA**; **hepatosplenomegaly.**\n\n### 3 — Level of consciousness\n- **⚠️⚠️ The book against its own vocabulary:** **IT IS MORE USEFUL TO DESCRIBE THE PATIENT'S SPONTANEOUS BEHAVIOUR AND RESPONSES TO STIMULI THAN TO USE TERMS SUCH AS *STUPOR* OR *OBTUNDED*.** **EVEN A COMA HAS A SPECTRUM OF POSSIBLE RESPONSES.** *`nr-coma` `co-2` prints the vocabulary it is warning against. **Both are transcribed.***\n- **What the scale is:** **the GCS demonstrates A HIERARCHY OF RESPONSES in each area, WHICH REFLECT THE SEVERITY OF THE COMA.**\n\n### ⚠️ THE GLASGOW COMA SCALE — every row, every point\n|**Response**|**Scale**|**Score**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**EYE OPENING**|**eyes open SPONTANEOUSLY**|**4**|\n|**EYE OPENING**|**eyes open TO VERBAL COMMAND, SPEECH OR SHOUT**|**3**|\n|**EYE OPENING**|**eyes open TO PAIN** *(not applied to face)*|**2**|\n|**EYE OPENING**|**NO eye opening**|**1**|\n|**VERBAL**|**ORIENTED**|**5**|\n|**VERBAL**|**CONFUSED CONVERSATION, but able to answer questions**|**4**|\n|**VERBAL**|**INAPPROPRIATE RESPONSES, words discernible**|**3**|\n|**VERBAL**|**INCOMPREHENSIBLE sounds or speech**|**2**|\n|**VERBAL**|**NO verbal response**|**1**|\n|**MOTOR**|**OBEYS COMMANDS for movement**|**6**|\n|**MOTOR**|**PURPOSEFUL MOVEMENT to painful stimulus** *(localises)*|**5**|\n|**MOTOR**|**WITHDRAWS from pain**|**4**|\n|**MOTOR**|**ABNORMAL (SPASTIC) FLEXION — DECORTICATE posture**|**3**|\n|**MOTOR**|**EXTENSOR (RIGID) response — DECEREBRATE posture**|**2**|\n|**MOTOR**|**NO motor response**|**1**|\n\n- **⚠️ The severity bands**, printed across the foot of the table: **MINOR BRAIN INJURY = 13–15 · MODERATE = 9–12 · SEVERE = 3–8.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ The number to remember:** the second figure's caption reads ***COMA < 8*.**\n- **⚠️ THE MINIMUM SCORE IS 3, NOT 0** — one point is scored in each domain even with no response at all.\n- **⚠️ MOTOR 3 AND 2 ARE `ca-5`'s POSTURES** — the scale embeds the localising signs, so **a GCS motor score of 3 or 2 is itself an anatomical statement.**\n\nSrc: book Neurology ch.11 printed p.109 (vital signs and general look) and p.110 (level of consciousness and both GCS tables, transcribed row by row), in full",
