        "id": "ca-4",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "The pupils and the eyes",
        "body": "### The pupils — the two autonomic lesions\n- **PARASYMPATHETIC LESIONS —** e.g. **OCULOMOTOR NERVE COMPRESSION in UNCAL HERNIATION or ANEURYSM.** **The pupil ENLARGES; ultimately, FULL DILATION with NO REACTION TO LIGHT.**\n- **SYMPATHETIC LESIONS —** **HORNER SYNDROME: MIOSIS, PTOSIS, ANHIDROSIS.**\n\n### Two pupil findings that name their own cause\n- **Pinpoint but reactive:** **→ PONTINE HAEMORRHAGE, or OPIATE INTOXICATION.**\n- **⚠️ BILATERAL FIXED, DILATED PUPILS — CONFIRM WITH BRIGHT LIGHT.** Consider:\n  - **a) ANTICHOLINERGIC DRUGS** — **ATROPINE · GLUTETHIMIDE · AMITRIPTYLINE · ANTIPARKINSONIAN AGENTS**\n  - **b) HYPOTHERMIA**\n  - **c) SEVERE BARBITURATE INTOXICATION**\n  - **⚠️⚠️ In red:** ***b and c CAN CAUSE A REVERSIBLE PICTURE SIMULATING BRAIN DEATH***\n  - **d) ONGOING OR RECENT SEIZURE** *(the pupil abnormality may be UNILATERAL)*\n  - **e) ANOXIC-ISCHAEMIC EVENT** — **FIXED PUPILS IMPLY POOR PROGNOSIS**\n\n### ⚠️ The pupil-size localisation figure — five levels\n|**Level**|**Pupils**|\n|---|---|\n|**METABOLIC**|**SMALL, REACTIVE to light**|\n|**DIENCEPHALON**|**SMALL, REACTIVE** — *bilateral Horner syndrome*|\n|**MIDBRAIN / TECTUM**|**MIDSIZE, NOT REACTIVE**|\n|**THIRD NERVE — UNCAL HERNIATION**|**LARGE, NOT REACTIVE**|\n|**PONS**|**PINPOINT, REACTIVE**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ Reactivity, not size:** **metabolic, diencephalic and pontine pupils are ALL REACTIVE** and range from pinpoint to small; **midbrain and third-nerve pupils are NOT.** *That is `nr-coma` `co-4`'s feature 3 — **pupillary reactions are usually preserved in metabolic coma** — turned into a figure.*\n\n### Eye movements\n- **OCULAR REFLEXES — OCULOCEPHALIC and OCULOVESTIBULAR — when PRESENT in a comatose patient → PRESERVED BRAINSTEM FUNCTION.**\n- *The doll's-eye strip: **patient focused on examiner's nose → after a sharp turn to the patient's right, the patient REMAINS focused on the examiner's nose** — with **corrective saccades** shown in the second row.*\n- **Dysconjugate at rest:** **→ PARESIS OF INDIVIDUAL MUSCLES, or INTERNUCLEAR OPHTHALMOPLEGIA.**\n- **⚠️ Conjugate lateral deviation:** toward one side indicates a lesion in **THE CONTRALATERAL BRAINSTEM**, or **THE IPSILATERAL CEREBRAL HEMISPHERE.**\n- **⚠️ Persistent rhythmical nystagmus:** **→ a SUBTLE FINDING OF NON-CONVULSIVE STATUS EPILEPTICUS** (`nr-coma` `co-8`).\n- **BLINKING:** **SPONTANEOUS BLINKING → INTACT ARAS.** **Locked-in syndrome → ONLY blinking and vertical eye movements.**\n\n### The conjugate gaze pathway, in the book's own six steps\n*To look to the RIGHT:*\n- **1 —** the **LEFT FRONTAL EYE FIELD (area 8)** fires; the signal **DECUSSATES** as it descends.\n- **2 —** it arrives at the **PARAMEDIAN PONTINE RETICULAR FORMATION (PPRF)**, the **\"HORIZONTAL GAZE CENTER\"**, in the **PONS.**\n- **3 —** the PPRF stimulates the **ABDUCENS NUCLEUS (CN VI) on the SAME side** — here the right. ⚠️ **The abducens nucleus houses TWO neuron types: LOWER MOTOR NEURONS to the RIGHT LATERAL RECTUS (abduction), and INTERNUCLEAR NEURONS.**\n- **4 —** the internuclear neurons **CROSS BACK over the midline** and ascend in the **MEDIAL LONGITUDINAL FASCICULUS (MLF).**\n- **5 —** the MLF delivers the signal to the **OCULOMOTOR NUCLEUS in the MIDBRAIN**, which activates the **LEFT MEDIAL RECTUS** (adduction).\n- **6 —** **both eyes move to the right in synchrony.**\n\n- **⚠️ Why INO is an MLF lesion:** the pathway above is the answer, and it is why **`nr-ms` and `nr-cranial` `cn-4` matter here** — *`cn-4` teaches III, IV and VI as one examination; this is the wiring underneath it.*\n\nSrc: book Neurology ch.11 printed p.111 (pupils, the five-level figure, ocular reflexes and the doll's-eye strip), p.112 (dysconjugate eyes, deviation, nystagmus and the six-step gaze box) and p.113 (the gaze figure and blinking) — all in full",
        "qs": []
